4601:
** σῑγάω**,
_Doric dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ σιγῇς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 778}}; Cyrenaic _infinitive_ σιγέν {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.170}}; _1st pers._ _singular_ _optative_ σιγῷμ (ι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 336}}: _future_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 113, 980, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 801, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1684, etc.}} later **-ήσω** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.27 (1st c.BC: Archias or Parmenio Epigrammaticus), 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 37.42, 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 1.10}}: _perfect_ σεσίγηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.218}}:— _passive_, _future_ σιγηθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1076; σεσιγήσομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 311c}}: _aorist_ ἐσιγήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 298, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.86:}}_perfect_ σεσίγημαι (see. below): (σιγή):— **keep silence**, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in _imperative_ σίγα}}, **hush! be still!** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.90, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.393; σιγᾶν “hymnus ad Mercurium” 93, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.61, 110; but frequently in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}}, Trag., and _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.29, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 200, etc.; σ. περί τινος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 312; πρὸς οὓς δεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 276a}}; πρὸς τοῦτο, ἐν τούτῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.20, “An.” 5.6.27.}}
**2** _metaphorically_ of things, σιγῶν δ᾽ ὄλεθρος καὶ μέγα φωνοῦντ᾽.. ἀμαθύνει [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 935 (_anapaest meter_); σύριγγες οὐ σιγῶσιν [prev. author] “Supp.” 181; σίγησε δ᾽ αἰθήρ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1084; σ. πόντος, σ. ἀῆται, ἁ δ᾽ ἐμὰ οὐ σ. ἀνία 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.38:—in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 781.13, τὰ σιγῶντ᾽ ὀνόματ᾽.. δαιμόνων seems to be ={τὰ ἄρρητα}}}], **secret, mystical**:—_passive_, μέμψομαι σιωπὴν ὡς ἐσιλγήθη κακῶς I shall impute as a fault that silence **was kept**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 298}}; also τί σεσίγηται δόμος Ἀδμήτο; why is it **all silent?** {{Refs|[prev. author] Alcaeus78}}; σιγῶντα λέγειν, λέγοντα σιγᾶν, phrases illustrating a logical fallacy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 300b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 166a13.}}
**II** _transitive_, **hold silent, keep secret**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 81, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 106, 441, “Ag.” 36, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.104 (assuming variant)}}, etc.:—_passive_, **to be kept silent** or **secret**, σεσιγαμένον χρῆμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.103; ὁ θάνατος.. ἐσιγήθη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.21; σιγώμενος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 653}}; ἐσιγάθη δ᾽ ἂν ὑφορβός **would never have been heard of**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.54.}}

4602:
** σῑγ-ή**,
_Doric dialect_ **σιγά**, ἡ, **silence**, σ. ἔχειν keep **silence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86}}; σ. ποιήσασθαι make **silence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.130; παρέχειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1115, etc.; σ. φυλάσσετε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 542; σ. τῶνδε θήσομαι πέρι [prev. author] “Med.” 66; γύναι, γυναιξὶ κόσμον ἡ σ. φέρει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 293; κόσμος ἡ σ. τε καὶ τὰ παῦρ᾽ ἔπη [prev. author] “fragment” 64; ὦ παῖ, σιώπα· πόλλ᾽ ἔχει σ. καλά [prev. author] “fragment” 81}}; πολλῶν φάρμακον κακῶν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Carcinus Tragicus 7.2}}; δυσμενὴς τῇ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.237}}; ἡ ἄγαν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1251, compare 1256: _plural_, σιγαὶ ἀνέμων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 10 (_anapaest meter_); σιγαὶ.. τῶν νεωτέρων παρὰ πρεσβυτέροις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 425b.}}
**b** in a mystical or religious sense,{{NT|Aristeas 95, NT.Rev.8.1; σ. σύμβολον θεοῦ ζῶντος “PMag.Par.” 1.559.}}
**II** σιγῇ, as _adverb_, **in silence**, the only case used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, πάντες ἥατο σιγῇ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.255, compare 3.8, +others}}; also διὰ σιγῆς, μετὰ σ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 450c, “Sph.” 264a; σὺν σιγῇ 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 25.22}}: like{σῖγα}, as an exclamation, σιγῇ νῦν **be silent now!** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.440.}}
**II.2** **in an undertone, in a whisper, secretly** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (σῖγα 2), σ. λόγον ἐποιέετο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.74; τὰ σ. βουλευόμενα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.19}}; σιγῇ ἔχειν τι keep it **secret**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.93}}; σιγᾷ καλύψαι, σιγῇ στέγειν, κεύθειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.7, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 341, “Tr.” 989 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.3** with _genitive_, σιγῇ τινος **unknown to** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.140, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 587.}}

4603:
** σῐδήρ-εος**, α,
_Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ η, ον, _Attic dialect_ _contraction_ **σιδηροῦς**, ᾶ, οῦν {{Refs|“SIG” 144.14, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ also **σιδήρειος**, η, ον, see below; also late, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Stud.Pal.” 20.217.9 (6th c.AD)}} (_feminine_ -ειος{{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Theognostus Grammaticus “Can.” 56)}}; _Doric dialect_ **σιδάρεος** [ᾱ] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).103.114 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}, and see below 11, also **σιδάριος** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 246 ii 67 (Delph., 4th c.BC)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **σιδάριος** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.24}}:—**made of iron** or **steel**, ἄξων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.723; σιδηρείη κορύνη 7.141; πύλαι 8.15; ὑποκρητηρίδιον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.25; σκύταλον 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 17.31}}; χεὶρ σ. **grappling-iron**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.25, 7.62: also σ. ὀρυμαγδός}}, i.e. the clang **of arms**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.424}}; σ. οὐρανός the **iron** sky, the firmament, which the ancients held to be of metal, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.329 (compare χάλκεος)}}; σ. γένος, of the {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Iron” age, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 176.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, ἦ γὰρ σοί γε σ. ἐν φρεσὶ θυμός a soul **of iron**, i.e. **hard, stubborn as iron**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.357, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.172; οὐδέ μοι.. θυμὸς ἐνὶ στήθεσσι σ., ἀλλ᾽ ἐλεήμων 5.191; οὐδ᾽ εἴ οἱ κραδίη γε σ. ἔνδοθεν ἦεν 4.293; σιδήρειόν νύ τοι ἦτορ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.205, 521}}; ἦ ῥά νυ σοί γε σ. πάντα τέτυκται thou art **iron** all! {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.280}}; πυρὸς μένος.. σ. the **iron** force of fire, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.177}}; of Heracles, the **ironsided**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 8}}; of men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 491; σὰρξ σ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.47}}; ὦ σιδήρεοι O ye **ironhearted!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.166; εἰ μὴ σιδηροῦς ἐστιν, οἴομαι ἔννουν γεγονέναι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.20; σ. λόγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 509a.}}
**II** σιδάρεοι, οἱ, **Byzantine iron coins**, always used in _Doric dialect_ form, even at Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 249, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 96, 5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 36.}}

4604:
** σίδηρος** [ῐ],
_Doric dialect_ **σίδᾱρος** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).102.61 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}, etc.: ; also ἡ, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 923}}: _neuter_ **σίδηρον**, τό, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia D 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.151, variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.65 and 3rd c.BC: Daimachus Historicus 4J.}} (but probably _falsa lectio_ for{σιδήριον} in [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.72, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {Ἀκίς}): _plural_ σίδηρα 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 2.12, “Etymologicum Magnum” 26.36, 12th c.AD: Tzetzes, Joannes Grammaticus (see. below)}}]: —**iron**, σ. πολιός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.366, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.168; ἰόεις 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.850; μέλας 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 151; αἴθων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.485, +others; πολύκμητος 6.48, +others, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.393}}; as an article of traffic, οἰνίζοντο.. Ἀχαιοί, ἄλλοι μὲν χαλκῷ, ἄλλοι δ᾽ αἴθωνι σ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.473; πλέων.. μετὰ χαλκόν· ἄγω δ᾽ αἴθωνα σίδηρον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.184}}; χαλκός τε χρυσός τε πολύκμητός τε σ., of treasures, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.133, +others}}; as a prize, {{Refs|23.261, 850}}; Σκύθης σ., because brought from the Euxine, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 818; ὁ πόντιος ξεῖνος.. θηκτὸς σ. [prev. work] 942 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** frequently as a symbol of **hardness** {{Refs|compare (σιδήρεος 1.2)}}, or of **stubborn force**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.372, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.494}}; ὀφθαλμοὶ ὡσεὶ κέρα ἕστασαν ἠὲ σ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|. [prev. work] 211; οὔ σφι λίθος χρὼς οὐδὲ σ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.510; ἐκ σ. κεχάλκευται.. καρδίαν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 123.4, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 658; ἦσθα πέτρος ἢ σ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1279 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 666c}}; also of **firmness, steadfastness**, πέτρης ὅ γ᾽ ἔχων νόον ἠὲ σ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 4.44, compare 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 5.22.}}
**II** **anything made of iron, iron tool** or **implement**, for husbandry, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.485, compare 23.834}}: also of weapons, **arrow-head**, {{Refs|4.123}}; **sword** or **knife**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|18.34, 23.30; αὐτὸς γὰρ ἐφέλκεται ἄνδρα σ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.294, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 966 (Lyric poetry)}}; **axe-head**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.587}}: generally, **arms**, οἱ Ἀθηναῖοι σ. κατέθεντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6; ὅπλοις τε καὶ σιδήρῳ διώξειν “OGI” 532.25 (Galatia, 1st c.BC)}}: also, **knife, sickle**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 387}}: _plural_, **fishing-hooks**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.49}}; **irons, fetters**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 2.12, 12th c.AD: Tzetzes, Joannes Grammaticus “Historiarum variarum chiliades” 13.302}}; compare σιδήριον.
**III** **place for selling iron, smithy** or **cutler's shop**, ἀγαγόντα εἰς τὸν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.7.}}

4606:
**Σιδώνιος**, -α, -ον
(Σιδών),
**of Sidon, Sidonian**;
(a) **the region**: sc. χώρα, Luk.4:26;
(b) **the people**: Act.12:20.†
(From Abbott-Smith. LSJ has no entry)

4613:
** Σίμων** [ῐ], ωνος, ὁ,
{{Refs|“Simon”, one of the Telchines (see. Τελχίν)}}, used proverbial of **a confederate in evil**, οἶδα Σίμωνα καὶ Σ. ἐμέ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 5.41.}}
**II** name of a throw of the dice, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 57.}}

4615:
** σῐνᾱπ-ῐ**, τό,
{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 1.45, “PFay.” 122.4, 12 (1st-2nd c.AD)}}; _genitive_ εως {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 9.13 (2nd c.BC), NT.Matt.13.31, “POxy.” 920.2 (2nd-3rd c.AD), 6th c.AD: Alexander Trallianus Medicus 1.12}}; ιος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 72.9 (3rd c.BC), “PStrassb.” 102.9 (3rd c.AD)}}; ις {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.453.6 (4th c.AD)}}; _dative_ ι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 20.21 (2nd c.AD)}}; also **σίνᾱπυ**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Diocl. fragment 120, “PCair.Zen.” 608.31, 703.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; _genitive_ υος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.15 codex}}; **σίνηπι**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.813, 1st-2nd c.AD: Crito Medicus [prev. work] 817, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.154, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 5.5, etc.}}; _genitive_ εως {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.248, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.38, “PLips.” 97 xxxiii 4 (4th c.AD), etc.}}; ιος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.2, variant in 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 4.3.32, etc.}}; **σίνηπυ**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 84, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Eup.” 1.14}}; also **σίνηπυς**, _accusative_ υν, ὁ, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 533}}, and **σίνᾱπις**, ἡ, Herod._middle_ in {{Refs|“Rh.Mus.” 58.88}}:— **mustard, Sinapis alba**:—in early _Attic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ none of these forms appear, but νᾶπυ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.54, “Morb.” 3.15, “Mul.” 1.13, compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.367a, 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 255.}}
**II** σίνηπι ἄγριον or Περσικόν,= θλάσπι, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.156.}}
**III** σ. κηπαῖον, **Brassica nigra**, {{Refs|[prev. work]154.}}

4616:
** σινδών**, όνος, ἡ
(ὁ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 153; _accusative_ _plural_ in 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus σινδούς, as εἰκούς from εἰκών)}}, **fine cloth**, usually **linen**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.200, 2.95, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus [same place], 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 210.67; βρόχῳ μιτώδει σινδόνος [prev. author] “Ant.” 1222}}; σ. βυσσίνης τελαμῶνες, used for mummies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.86}}; of surgeons' bandages, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.181 (but also ἐξ ἐρίου τὰς σ. ὑφαίνουσιν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.7.7, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.20).}}
**2** **anything made of such cloth, garment of linen** (sometimes **muslin**), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Michel” 832.19 (Samos, 4th c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 176.255 (3rd c.BC), “SIG” 2754.5 (Pergam.), “PTeb.” 182 (2nd c.BC), “UPZ” 84.4 (2nd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Deor.Conc.” 10}}; ἐν εὐτελεῖ σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.340d}}; **napkin**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.66}}; **ship's sail**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 773.42 (Lyric poetry), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “epigram” 39, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.12}}; **flag, standard**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.66.10}}; **cloth** or **sheet**, σ. καθαρά {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.46.206 (4th c.AD)}} (so of a winding-sheet, {{NT|NT.Matt.27.59, compare “PPar.” 18b is 10); σ. κοιτάριαι}}**sheets,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Edict.Diocl. 28.16, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49}}; ἐντὸς σινδόνος within the **veil, esoteric**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 17.72}}; ἔξω σ. **exoteric**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 18.89.}}

4621:
** σῖτος**, ὁ,
irregular _plural_ σῖτα, τά, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 2.8, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.128, 5.34 (_neuter_ _sing._ σῖτον only “Delph.” 3(5).3 ii 19 (4th c.BC))}}:— **grain**, comprehending both wheat (πυρός) and barley (κριθή), ἐν [Ἰθάκῃ] σ. ἀθέσφατος ἐν δέ τε οἶνος γίγνεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.244}}; περὶ σίτου ἐκβολήν about the shooting of **the corn** into ear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.1}}; τοῦ σ. ἀκμάζοντος at **its** ripening, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.19; πρὶν τὸν σ. ἐν ἀκμῇ εἶναι [prev. author] 4.2; τὸν νέον σ. σὺν τῇ καλάμῃ ἀποκείμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.27}}; σ. ἀληλεσμένος or -εμένος ground **corn**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.23, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26; σ. ἀπηλοημένος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.6; σῖτον ἐσαγαγεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.6, etc.; σ. ἐπείσακτος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.87}}; σίτου εἰσαγωγή, ἐξαγωγή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1133b9, “IG” 12.57.35; συγκομιδή 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.14; ἐγδοχεία “PMich.Zen.” 23 (3rd c.BC)}}; comprehending πυρός, κριθή, ὄλυρα, and φακός, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 66.41 (2nd c.BC)}}; περὶ τοῦ σ. καὶ τοῦ σησάμου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 43.3 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὁ σ. καὶ τὰ λάχανα as examples of πόα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.3.1.}}
**2** **food made from grain, bread**, opposed to flesh-meat, σ. καὶ κρέα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.9, 12.19, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.168}}; σῖτον ἔδοντες, a general epithet of men as opposed to to beasts, ὅσσοι νῦν βροτοί εἰσιν ἐπὶ χθονὶ σ. ἔδ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.222, compare 9.89}}; of savages, who eat flesh only, οὐδέ τι σῖτον ἤσθιον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 146}}; of civilized men, σῖτον καὶ σπείρουσι καὶ σιτέονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.17; σωρὸν σίτου κεχυμένον [prev. author] 1.22; ἐσθίειν ἐπὶ τῷ σ. ὄψον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.14.2}}; κάρδαμον ἔχειν ἐπὶ τῷ σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.2.11}}; πίνειν ὕδωρ ἐπὶ τῷ σ{{Refs 1st c.AD+|. [prev. work] 6.2.27, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 29, with [prev. author] 2.328f.}}
**3** in a wider sense, **food**, as opposed to to drink, σ. ἠδὲ ποτής {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.87, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.306; σ. καὶ οἶνος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.479, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.706; σ. καὶ μέθυ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.746, etc.}}; even of porridge (κυκεών),{{Refs 8th c.BC+|10.235; σῖτα καὶ ποτά 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.27; σ. ποιεῖν καὶ οἶνον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372a; ἄκμηνος σίτοιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.163, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 182}}; εὐνὴ καὶ σ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.130, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.129; ὕπνον καὶ σ. αἱρεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.75}}; **provisions**, σῖτα ἀναιρέεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.128; παρέχειν σῖτα καὶ νέας [prev. author] 7.21}}; παρέχειν μέχρι τριάκοντα ἡμερῶν σ. Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.47.}}
**4** rarely of beasts, **fodder**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 604, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 383 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 4.1.}}—In the general sense of **food**, Prose writers prefer the diminutive form σιτία, τά.
**II** in _Attic dialect_ Law, **allowance of grain** made to widows and orphans. σῖτον διδόναι, ἀποδιδόναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.15, 28.11, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 56.7.}}
**II.2** δίκην σίτου δικάσασθαι, bring an action under the Athen. Corn-law against regraters and monopolists, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.9, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.52.}}
**II.3** **allowance** made to the Ἱππεῖς, {{Refs|“IG” 12.304.4, +others}}
**II.4** **public distribution of corn** in Rome, Latin _frumentatio_, τὸν ἐπὶ τοῦ σίτου ὄντα ἐν Ῥώμῃ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.10.2.}}

4622:
**Σιών **
indecl.,
in NT anarth., but in LXX when used of the city of Jerusalem, ἡ Σ.
(Heb. צִיּוֹן),
**Zion**;
1. **the mountain**: in typical sense, of the Church, Heb.12:22; of heaven, Rev.14:1.
2. The city, i.e. Jerusalem; in poetical sense;
(a) **of the inhabitants**: θυγάτηρ Σ, Mat.21:5, Jhn.12:15" (LXX) ;
(b) **in wider sense, of Israel**: Rom.11:26;
(with) figuratively: τίθήμι ἐν Σ λίθον, Rom.9:33, 1Pe.2:6" (LXX) .†
(From Abbott-Smith. LSJ has no entry)

4623:
** σῐωπ-άω**,
_infinitive_ σιωπᾶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.280:}}_future_ -ήσομαι in early writers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 233, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 309, “Av.” 225, “Lys.” 364, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 234a, etc.}} later **-ήσω** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator “Epistulae” 10.1, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.240e, etc. (compare σιγάω)}}: _aorist_ (ἐ) σιώπησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.568, etc.}}: _perfect_ σεσιώπηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 944, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.34}}:—_middle_ and _passive_, see below: _Doric dialect_ **σωπάω** (which see):—**keep silence**, σιωπᾶν λαὸν ἀνώγει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.280, compare 23.568, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.513, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10, etc.; Σιμωνίδης τὴν ζωγραφίαν ποίησιν σιωπῶσαν προσαγορεύει 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.346f}}; φησὶν σιωπῶν his silence is an admission, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1592, compare “IA” 1245; πονηρῶν ἔργων δόξει κοινωνεῖν τῷ σιωπῆσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.33}}; σ. τινί **keep silence for** or **at the behest of.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1134, “Lys.” 530; σ. πρός τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 234a; πρὸς τοῦτο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.20; ὑπέρ τινος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 796; _imperative_ σιώπα}}**hush! be still!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 81, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 529, etc.}}
**2** of bees, **to be still**, opposed to βομβέω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 627a24.}}
**II** _transitive_, **keep secret, speak not of**, τὰ δίκαια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1037, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 27, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 6.10, etc.; σ. ὅτι.. “PMasp.” 295.21 (5th c.AD)}}:—_passive_, ἂν σιωπηθῇ τὰ παρὰ τῶν πολεμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 21, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.22, etc.}}; τί σιγῶσ᾽ ὧν σιωπᾶσθαι χρεώ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 432; σιωπώμενον καὶ ἀβασάνιστον ἐᾶσαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.13; οὐ τὸ αἰσχρὸν σιωπηθήσεται 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.155; ταῦτα σιωπᾶσθαι συνέφερεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.42; ἡ σιωπωμένη ἀλήθεια 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.76.}}
**III** _middle_, **silence**, σιωπησάμενος τὰ πλήθη {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.46.4.}}

4625:
Included with: ** σκανδαλ-ιστής**, οῦ, ὁ,
probably **acrobat who performed on a trapeze** (σκάνδαλον), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“SIG” 847.5 (Delph., 2nd c.AD), see “Supp.epigram” 2.328. σκάνδαλον, τό}}, **trap** or **snare** laid for an enemy, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Josh.23.13, LXX.1Ki.18.21, NT.Rom.11.9, NT.1Pet.2.7}}; probably laid for animals, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 608.7 (3rd c.BC, written σκανδάνων, _genitive_ _plural_)}}: _metaphorically_, **stumbling-block, offence, scandal,** {{NT+6th c.AD+|NT.Matt.18.7, NT.Luke.17.1; σκάνδαλα ποιεῖσθαι “PMasp.” 4.9 (6th c.AD).}}
**II** see at {σκανδαλιστής}.

4626:
** σκάπτω**,
{{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 90, etc.}}: _future_ σκάψω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 778e, (κατα-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 566}}: _aorist_ ἔσκαψα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 12, (κατ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.156, etc.:}}_perfect_ ἔσκᾰφα (κατ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.7,35:}} —_passive_, _future_ σκᾰφήσομαι (ἀπο-) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 5.10.3, (κατα-) 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 20.6.1:}}_aorist_ ἐσκάφην [ᾰ] {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Isa.5.6, “Geoponica” 12.5.1, (κατ-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 22, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἔσκαμμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 413a, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 6}}:—**dig**, _absolutely_, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 778e; σ. τἆλλά τε μοχθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 525: proverbial, σ. οὐκ ἐπίσταμαι [prev. author] “Av.” 1432, compare “fragment” 221, NT.Luke.16.3}}:—_middle_, σ. δικέλλῃ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 158.}}
**II** with _accusative_,
**II.1** **dig, delve**, for cultivation, σκάπτων, ἀρῶν γῆν, ποιμνίοις ἐπιστατῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 188, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 16.15; τοὺς ἀμπελῶνας 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.31; ὑπόλιθον γῄδιον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 31.}}
**II.2** **dig about, cultivate by digging**, φυτὰ σ. (as we say **to hoe** turnips) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 90, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.20: _metaphorically_, σκάπτει, μοχλεύει θύρετρα}} **digs about** them, **digs** them **up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 999.}}
**II.3** of the result, σ. τάφρον **dig** a trench, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.90}}; σ. βαθεῖαν (i.e. τάφρον) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.20.4; θεμελίους 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 10:}} —_passive_, τὰ ἐσκαμμένα,= σκάμμα {{Refs|11.2, hence, _metaphorically_, ὑπὲρ τὰ ἐσκαμμένα ἅλλεσθαι}} to leap **too far**, or **further than seemed possible**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 413a, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 6, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 438, “Or.” 64.69 (variant{ὑπὲρ τὸ σκάμμα});compare σκάμμα 11, ὑποσκάπτω.}}]

4627:
** σκᾰφ-η**, ἡ:
**I** **trough, tub, basin**, or **bowl** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in _diminutive_ σκαφίς), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.73, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 742, etc.}}; **kneading-trough** or **baker's tray**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 33, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 10.102}}; **wash-tub, bath**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 225, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Steril.” 234}}; **bowl** or **tray** on which offerings were carried by metoeci at the Panathenaea, etc., {{Refs|“IG” 12.844.6, 22.1388.46, +others, Semus 5}}; compare σκαφηφόρος: proverbial, τὰ σῦκα σῦκα, τὴν σκάφην σκάφην λέγει {{Refs 1st c.AD+|15th c.AD: Apostolius Paroemiographus 15.95b, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.178b, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 41, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.208a.}}
**I.2** **light boat, skiff**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1315}} (with a pun on _signification_ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1), “PCair.Zen.” 25.5 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.23.7}}; used for cargo, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1742.9 (1st c.BC)}}; **boat-load**, ξύλων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PGrad.” 9.5 (3rd c.BC).}}
**I.3** child's **cradle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1454b25, 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 36 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 3, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.106, +others, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 138 (probably).}}
**I.4** **grave**, {{Refs|“BCH” 24.394 (from Bithynia).}}
**II** **concave sun-dial**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 9.8.1, 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.10, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.110}}. (Prob. originally **something dug** or **scooped out**, from σκάπτω.)

4628:
** σκέλος**, εος, τό,
**leg** from the hip downwards, only once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, πρυμνὸν σκέλος}} the ham or buttock, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.314; κάμηλος ἐν τοῖσι ὀπισθίοισι σ. ἔχει τέσσερας μηροὺς καὶ γούνατα τέσσερα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.103, compare 7.61, 88}}; τὰ σκέλη τε καὶ τὰ ἰσχία πρὸς τὴν γῆν ἐρείσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 494a4}}; of dancers, τὸ σ. ῥίψαντες, αἴρειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 332, “Ec.” 265; σ. οὐράνιον ἐκλακτίζων [prev. author] “V.” 1492, compare 1526}}; οὐρανῷ σκέλη προφαίνων, of one thrown head foremost, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 753}}; βαδιοῦνται ἐπὶ δυοῖν σκελοῖν, ἐφ᾽ ἑνὸς πορεύσονται σκέλους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190d}}; ὁ δεινός, ὁ ταλαύρινος, ὁ κατὰ τοῖν σκελοῖν he **with the legs, the strider**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 241 (but explained by Scholia ἀπὸ τῶν διὰ δειλίαν ἀποτιλώντων,compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περινθία” 18); dual, τὼ σκέλει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 325, +others, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 26, “Anach.” 1}}; σκέλε (i.e. probably σκέλει) δύο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.1388.24, compare 1502.5; but σκέλη (_plural_) δύο in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. from 4th c.BC, [prev. work]1425.15, compare 57, etc.; and so τὰ σ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 9}}: _singular_, **leg** of sacrificial victim, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.190.32, +others,42(1).40.10 (Epid., 5th c.BC).}}
**2** as a military phrase, ἐπὶ σκέλος πάλιν χωρεῖν, ἀνάγειν, retreat with the face towards the enemy, retire leisurely, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1400, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 383; compare πούς 1.6b.}}
**3** κατὰ σκέλος βαδίζειν, of the lion and the camel, **with the hind foot following the fore on the same side** (not crosswise), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 498b7, compare 629b14.}}
**4** παρὰ σκέλος ἀπαντᾷ it meets one **across**, i.e. crosses one's path, thwarts one, [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.12.2 (variant{π. μέλος}).}}]
**II** _metaphorically_, τὰ σ. the **legs**, i.e. the **two long walls** connecting Athens with Piraeus, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.1.15, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cimon” 13}}; τὰ μακρὰ σ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.107, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 14}}; of the long walls between Megara and Nisaea, τὰ Μεγαρικὰ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1170; between Corinth and Lechaeum, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.6.22.}}
**II.b** **side-wall** of a temple, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 247 K 1 iii 3, 11 (Delph., 4th c.BC)}}; of other structures, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.88 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**II.2** **side-poles** or **frames of an engine**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 49.4.4.}}
**II.3** **tails of a surgical bandage**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 48.20.5}}; of the **ends** of the Persian head-dress, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.820d.}}
**II.4** **members** of a sentence, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Scholia recentia 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 94}}. (Written **σχέλος** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 11(2).161 B 61 (Delos, 3rd c.BC).)}}

4631:
** σκευ-ή**, ἡ,
**equipment, attire, apparel**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.15, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 555, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 180, etc.; σ. Μηδικὰς ἐνδυόμενος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130; σκευήν τινα περιθέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 53d}}; σκευῆς ἀνάθεσις, of the chorus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.4}}; especially of the **dress** of a singer or actor, ἐνδὺς πᾶσαν τὴν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 108}}; τραγικὴ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 577b}}; of soldiers, σ. ψιλή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.94; ἡ σ. τῶν ὅπλων [prev. author] 1.8}}; of **horse-trappings**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.94}}; of the **dress** of priests and public officers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.112, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 71.}}
**2** **fashion, style of dress** or **equipment**, Μηδικὴ αὕτη ἡ σ. ἐστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.62; τὴν αὐτὴν σ. ἔχοντες [prev. work] 66, compare 73, +others}}; ἐπὶ πολὺ αὕτη ἡ σ. κατέσχεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6.}}
**II** **tackle**, as of a net, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.80}}; of a ship, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.79, NT.Act.27.19.}}
**II.2** ={αἰδοῖον}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.241 (3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus, where σκεϋήν [musical notation], uncertain reading);compare σκεῦος 111.}}

4632:
** σκεῦος**, εος, τό,
**vessel** or **implement of any kind**, in _singular_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 402, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.128; in dual, σκεύη δύο χρησίμω 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 983, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 596b; and in _plural_, κλῖναι καὶ.. τἆλλα σκεύη [prev. work] 373a, +others}}:—but the _plural_ is frequently used in a collective sense, **all that belongs to a complete outfit, house-gear, utensils, chattels**, opposed to live-stock and fixtures, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1318, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.31, etc.}}; σ. γεωργικά farming **implements**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 552}}; ἱερὰ σ. sacred **vessels and implements**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, compare “IG” 12.313.20}}; a druggist's **stores**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.17.3}}; σ. τὰ ἐπιτράπεζα table-**furniture**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lap.” 42}}; military **accoutrements, equipment**, τὰ περὶ τὸ σῶμα σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31}}; τὰ τῶν ἵππων σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.55}}; **baggage** of an army, and, generally, **baggage, luggage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 12, 15, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.13.6}}; ὄνοι αὐτοῖς σκεύεσι **packs** and all, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 5.4.17}}; **tackle, gear** of ships, **naval stores**, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.74.14, 22.1611.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 117d, “La.” 183e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.11, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 46.1; σ. τριηρικά 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.19}}; τὰ σκέα (σκεύη) τοῦ πλοίου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.437.2 (3rd c.BC)}} (so, collectively, in _singular_, {{NT|NT.Act.27.17): various kinds of σκεύη catalogued by Pollux (10).}}
**2** **inanimate object, thing**, opposed to ζῷον, σῶμα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 601d, “Grg.” 506d}}; opposed to ὄργανον, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 159}}; Protagoras gave the name of σκεύη to _neuter_ nouns, ἄρρενα καὶ θήλεα καὶ σκεύη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1407b8}}; ὑπηρετικὸν σ. a subordinate **person, a mere tool** or **chattel**, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.5.7; σ. ἀγχίνουν καὶ πολυχρόνιον [prev. author] 15.25.1: in “NT”, in good sense, σ. ἐκλογῆς}} a chosen **instrument**, of Paul, {{NT|NT.Act.9.15.}}
**II** τὸ σ. the **body**, as the **vessel** of the soul, a _metaphorically_ clearly expressed in {{NT|NT.2Cor.4.7, ἔχομεν δὲ τὸν θησαυρὸν τοῦτον ἐν ὀστρακίνοις σκεύεσιν,cf. NT.1Thes.4.4, NT.1Pet.3.7.}}
**III** ={αἰδοῖον}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“APl.” 4.243 (1st c.AD: Antistius Epigrammaticus), 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 17.11.}}
**IV** **sarcophagus,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Jahresh. 26 “Beibl.” 13 (Ephesus, 2nd c.AD).}}

4633:
** σκην-ή**,
_Doric dialect_ **σκᾱνά**, ἡ (not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, who uses κλισίη (which see))}}.
**I** **tent, booth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.314.110, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1289; ἐπὶ σκηναῖς.. ναυτικαῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 3; σκηνῆς ἔνδον [prev. work] 218 (_anapaest meter_); ὑπὸ σκηναῖσι [prev. work] 754}}; σκηνῆς ὕπαυλος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 796; σκηνὴν ποιήσαντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34; πηξάμενοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.12, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.30; ἵστασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.3}}; τὰς σ. καταλύειν, διαλύειν, strike **camp**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.40.2, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.25.3; σ. δερματίνη “PCair.Zen.” 13.14 (3rd c.BC)}}; but also σ. μάλα ἰσχυρῶν ξύλων **hut**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 7.23}}; **booth** in the marketplace, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 658, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.169 (both _plural_), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.16; σκανὰν ἐμ Πυλαίᾳ τὰν πρώταν ὑπάρχειν αὐτῷ “SIG” 422.11 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}: _plural_, **camp**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 686, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 731, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.7.}}
**I.2** σκηνὰς ἐς ἱεράς to the holy **tabernacle**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 806, compare 1129, LXX.Exo.26.1, +others}}
**II** **stage-building** as background for plays, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 817c, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.123 ff., 1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 5.6.1; τῆς σ. τὸ τέγος “IG” 11(2).161 A 115, cf. D 127 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), 153.14 ([prev. passage])}}; τραγικὴ σ. **a sort of** πῆγμα, such as that from which the prologue of [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ag.” is perhaps spoken, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.54, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 44, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {τραγικὴ σ}.}}]
**II.2** οἱ ἀπὸ τῆς σ. [ἥρωες] **heroes represented on the stage**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.180}}; οἱ ἀπὸ σκηνῆς **actors, players**, opposed to χορός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 922b17; also οἱ περὶ σκηνήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 16; οἱ ἐπὶ σκηνῆς 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.65 codices, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 16; compare σκηνικός and see below 111.1b.}}
**II.3** τὸ ἐπὶ τῆς σκηνῆς μέρος that which is **actually represented on the stage**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1459b25}}; τὰ ἀπὸ τῆς σκηνῆς (i.e. ᾄσματα), **songs** or **odes sung by one of the actors standing on the stage** (not by the chorus),{{Refs|[prev. work] 1452b18; τὰ μὲν ἀπὸ τῆς σ. οὐκ ἀντίστροφα, τὰ δὲ τοῦ χοροῦ ἀντίστροφα [prev. author] “Pr.” 918b27.}}
**II.4** _metaphorically_, **stage-effect, acting, unreality**, σκηνὴ πᾶς ὁ βίος 'all the world's a **stage',** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 10.72 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus); ἡ σ. τοῦ βίου 2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 7.10}}; **theatrical trick, deception**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.21.2, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.12.3.}}
**III** **tented cover, tilt of a wagon** or **carriage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.11, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.25, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 26; σ. τροχήλατοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 1000 (Lyric poetry)}}; also, **bed-tester**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.11.}}
**III.b** _metaphorically_, τὸν ὑπὸ (probably conjecture for ἐπὶ) σκηνῆς βίον the **hidden** life, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 21.}}
**III.2** in large ships, **state-cabin** on the poop, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.89, 4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 29; τῶν συριῶν ὑπὲρ τὴν σ. οὐσῶν “PHib.” 1.38.7 (3rd c.BC); ἀποκαταστήσω [τὸν σῖτον] ἐπὶ σκηνήν [prev. work] 86.8 (3rd c.BC).}}
**IV** **entertainment given in tents, banquet**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.1, 4.2.34, etc.; σ. δημοσία [prev. author] “Lac.” 15.4.}}

4634:
** σκηνο-πηγία**, ἡ,
**setting up of tents: nest-building**, ἡ τῆς χελιδόνος σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 612b22.}}
**2** **feast of tents** or **tabernacles**, {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Deu.16.16, LXX.2Mac.1.9, NT.John.7.2, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.4.1, +others}}; σύλλογος τῆς σ. {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 5361 (Egypt, Jewish).}}

4635:
** σκηνοποι-ός**, ὁ,
**tentmaker,** {{NT|NT.Acts.18.3.}}
**II** **maker of stage-properties,** {{Refs|Comica Adespota 98.}}
**III** (σκῆνος {{Refs|11)}} **making bodies**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.69.}}

4636:
** σκῆνος**,
_Doric dialect_ **σκᾶνος**, εος, τό,= σκηνή, **hut, tent**, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3071 (from Teos).}}
**II** **the body** (as the **tabernacle** of the soul), {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ καρδίης” 7, “Anatomy texts” 1, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 37, 187, 223, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 366a, 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 100a, 101c, 101e, NT.2Cor.5.1}}; σ. [μελίσσης] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.404 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.2** **dead body, corpse,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 3.1330, 12(5).591 (from Ceos), “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3123 (from Teos)}}, etc.; of an animal, μόσχου, ταύρων, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 447, “Th.” 742 (_plural_).}}

4637:
** σκην-όω**,
**pitch tents, encamp**, ἐσκήνωσαν variant for{-ησαν} in [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.14; σκηνοῖεν variant for{σκηνῷεν} (conjecture) in [prev. work] 7.4.12.}}]
**2** ={σκηνέω} (which see entry near the end), **live** or **dwell in a tent**, ἐν τῷ ὁμοῦ σκηνοῦν probably conjecture in {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.1.25}}: generally, **settle, take up one's abode**, κατὰ τὰς κώμας σκηνοῦν {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 4.5.23}}; -οῦν ἐν ταῖς οἰκίαις{{LXX+1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 5.5.11; ἐν τῇ ἀκροπόλει, οὗπερ αὐτὸς ἐσκήνου [prev. author] “HG” 5.4.56, cf. LXX.Judg.5.17, +others, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.12.6: _metaphorically_, ὁ λόγος.. ἐσκήνωσεν ἐν ἡμῖν NT.John.1.14}}:—hence in _perfect_ _passive_, **live** or **be**, πόρρω ἐσκήνωται (variant{ἐσκήνηται}) τοῦ θανάσιμος εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 610e.}}
**II** _transitive_, **pitch** a tent, σκηνὰς.. σκηνώσας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 7.21.6.}}
**II.2** τὸν τόπον τὸν νῦν σκενοῖ (sic) the place which he now **inhabits**, uncertain in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 499.89 (3rd c.BC).}}

4638:
** σκήν-ωμα**, ατος, τό,
={σκήνημα}, mostly in _plural_, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 616, “Ion” 1133, “Cyc.” 324, LXX.2Ki.7.23, +others, 2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 43, etc.}}; soldiers' **quarters**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.16}}: _singular_, **tent**, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.4.10, +others}}
**2** in _singular_ _metaphorically_,= σκῆνος {{NT|11, NT.2Pet.1.13; τὸ σ. τῆς ψυχῆς Sextus Pythagoreus “Sent.” 320.}}
**3** **temple**, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.14(15).1, +others}}: name of a building at Sparta, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.17.6.}}
**4** = **papilio,** {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}} (perhaps in both senses, **pavilion** and **butterfly**, compare σκῆν).

4639:
** σκῐά**, ᾶς,
_Ionic dialect_ **σκῐή**, ῆς, ἡ, **shadow**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.207}}; σκιὰ ἀντίστοιχος ὥς like the **shadow** that is one's double, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 745; ὑπὸ κίονος σκιὰν ἔπτηξεν [prev. author] “HF” 973: proverbial, τὴν αὑτοῦ σ. δέδοικεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 77, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 101d.}}
**2** **reflection, image** (in a bowl of oil), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.755.}}
**3** **shade** of one dead, **phantom**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.495, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 992 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1257; σποδόν τε καὶ σκιάν [prev. author] “El.” 1159}}; κατθανὼν δὲ πᾶς ἀνὴρ γῆ καὶ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 532; σκιᾷ τινι λόγους ἀνέσπα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 301}}; also, of **one worn to a shadow**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 302}}; κακωθεὶς δ᾽ οὐδὲν ἄλλ᾽ εἰμ᾽ ἢ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Niob.” in “Bull.Soc.Alex.” No.; φωνὴ καὶ σ. γέρων ἀνήρ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 509}}: frequently in proverbs of man's mortal estate, σκιᾶς ὄναρ ἄνθρωπος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.95; εἴδωλον σκιᾶς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 839, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 659.6}}; ὁρῶ γὰρ ἡμᾶς οὐδὲν ἄλλο πλὴν εἴδωλα.. ἢ κούφην σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 126; ἄνθρωπός ἐστι πνεῦμα καὶ σ. μόνον [prev. author] “fragment” 13; οὐδέν ἐσμεν πλὴν σκιαῖς ἐοικότες [prev. author] “fragment” 945}}; of human affairs, εὐτυχοῦντα μὲν σκιᾷ τις ἂν πρέψειεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1328 (uncertain reading)}}; οὐδὲν μᾶλλον ἢ καπνοῦ σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 399; καπνοῦ σκιὰν δέδοικεν “Comica Adespota” 692}}; of worthless things, τἄλλ᾽ ἐγὼ καπνοῦ σκιᾶς οὐκ ἂν πριαίμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1170, compare “Ph.” 946; καπνοὺς καὶ σκιάς 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 51; ὅσ᾽ ἂν γένηται ταῦτα πάντ᾽ ὄνου σκιά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 331}}; περὶ ὄνου σκιᾶς [μάχεσθαι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 191, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 260c; 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus}} wrote a Comedy texts entitled Ὄνου σκι; ἡ ἐν Δελφοῖς σ. that **phantom** at Delphi, of the Amphictyonic council, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.25}}; αἱ τοῦ δικαίου σ. **mere shadows** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 517d; σκιαὶ καὶ ἐν ὕδασιν εἰκόνες [prev. work] 510e}}; σκιαὶ τῶν ὄντων, ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ εἰδώλων σ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|. [prev. work] 532c; στιγμὴ ἢ σ. τούτων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.115; ἂν ἔχῃ φίλου σκιάν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 554.}}
**4** **evil spirit,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|Hippiatr. 130, “PMasp.” 188.5 (6th c.AD).}}
**II** **shade** of trees, etc., as a protection from heat, πετραίη τε σκιή the **shade** of a rock, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 589}}; ἐν σκιῇ ἑζόμενος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 593; ἐν συμμιγεῖ σκιᾷ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 239c; εἰ ὑπὸ σκιῇ ἔσοιτο ἡ μάχη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.226; ὑπὸ σκιᾶς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 458; εἰσελθὼν ὑπὸ τὴν σκιὰν καθέζεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.38; θέρους σκιὰν παρέχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 76d}}; ἐν σκιᾷ, i.e. indoors, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.18, compare “Cyn.” 3.3}}; σκιὰν ὑπερτείνασα Σειρίου κυνός **shade** from its heat, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 967: _plural_, αἱ τῶν δένδρων, αἱ τῶν πετρῶν σ., 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.17; ὑπὸ σκιαῖς [prev. author] “Oec.” 20.18, compare 5.9.}}
**III** **shadow in painting**, τὰ λαμπρὰ τῇ σκιᾷ τρανότερα ποιοῦσι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.863e, compare 407a, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isaeo” 4, 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 17.3}}; ἀνθρώπων πρῶτος ἐξευρὼν φθορὰν καὶ ἀπόχρωσιν σκιᾶς, of the painter Apollodorus, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.346a, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**III.2** **silhouette, profile**, Διόδωρος σ. Ἀντιφίλου ἐποίησεν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 344 (Alexandria, 2nd c.BC).}}
**III.3** perhaps **coloured border** on a garment, καλάσηριν ἢ ὑπόδυμα μὴ ἔχον σκιάς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.19, compare 24 (Andania, 1st c.BC),compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 561, “BGU” 1141.41, 43 (1st c.BC).}}
**IV** **an uninvited guest, introduced by another** (Latin _umbra_), {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.707a, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 110}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _chāyā´_ 'shadow'.)

4640:
** σκιρτ-άω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **σκιρτ-έω** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 4.342:}} — _frequentative verb_ of σκαίρω, **spring, leap, bound**, of young horses, αἱ δ᾽ ὅτε μὲν σκιρτῷεν ἐπὶ ζείδωρον ἄρουραν.., ἀλλ᾽ ὅτε δὴ σ. ἐπ᾽ εὐρέα νῶτα θαλάσσης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.226, 228; πῶλοι ἐσκίρτων φόβῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1125}}; of goats, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.152}}; of the Bacchae, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 446; ὀρχεῖσθε καὶ σ. καὶ χορεύετε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 761, compare “V.” 1305}}; ἅλλεσθαι καὶ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 653e}}: also of wind, σκιρτᾷ δ᾽ ἀνέμων πνεύματα πάντων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1085 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **to be skittish, unruly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 362.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 571c, etc.}}

4642:
** σκληρός**, ά, όν,
also _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.29, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [288]}}, hyperdorian mode **σκλᾱρός** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 104c}}:— **hard**, opposed to μαλακός in all senses:
**I** **hard** to the touch, ξύλον σ. ἢ μαλακόν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1194}}; ἐλαία Pi.[same place]; γῆ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 319, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 16.11; κοίτη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 942d, etc.}}
**I.2** of sound, **harsh**, σκληρὸν ἐβρόντησε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 839; βρονταί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.12; ἡ φωνὴ σκληροτέρα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 801b38, +others}}
**I.3** of taste and smell, **harsh, bitter**, σ. ὕδατα (springing from a rocky soil) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 1; so σκληρότατος ἀὴρ καὶ τόπος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.21.5}}; of wine, **dry**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 579, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Alex.Praef.”; ὀσμαί 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.14.12 (_comparative_)}}: _metaphorically_, σ. φράσις {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 2.}}
**I.4** **stiff, unyielding**, opposed to ὑγρός (lithe and supple), τιτθία σ. καὶ κυδώνια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1199; σκληρότεροι μαστοί 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 688a27; σκέλη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.6; τί τὸ ὑγρὸν τοῦ χαλινοῦ καὶ τί τὸ σ. [prev. work] 10.10}}; of the hair (compare σκληρόθριξ), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 517b11 (_comparative_), +others}}; σ. δέρμα, σάρξ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “PA” 665a2, “Phgn.” 806b22, etc.}}; of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 162b, “Smp.” 196a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 13, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 21}}; of dogs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 3.2; τράχηλος [prev. work] 5.30}}; οἱ τὸ σῶμα σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 873a34, +others}}
**I.5** κοιλίη σ. **costive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.25, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 670b9.}}
**I.6** of light, **strong**, ἐν σ. αὐγῇ ἢ μαλακῇ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Col.” 793b17.}}
**I.7** of a wind, **strong,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Jam.3.4, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.110, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 9.57.}}
**II** _metaphorically_,
**II.1** of things, **hard, austere**, μὴ τὰ μαλακὰ μῶσο, μὴ τὰ σ. ἔχῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [same place]; τροφή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1615; δίαιται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 525.5; βίος 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 522}}; τὰ σ. **hard words**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1406; σ. συμφοραί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 684.3; σκληρὰ μαλθακῶς λέγων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 774}}; τόνος ἀπηνὴς καὶ σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 2}}; τὸ σ. ={σκληρότης}, ἡ δίαιτα.. ὑπερβάλλει ἐπὶ τὸ σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1270b33.}}
**II.2** of persons, **harsh, austere, cruel, stubborn**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 24.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 155e, 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus [same place]}}; σ. ἀοιδός, of the Sphinx, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 36; σ. γὰρ αἰεί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 500; ὦ σ. δαῖμον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1264; τοὺς τρόπους σκληρός [prev. author] “Pax” 350}}; ἄγροικοι καὶ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1128a9; σ. ψυχή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1361, “Tr.” 1260 (_anapaest meter_); σ. ἄγαν φρονήματα [prev. author] “Ant.” 473; ἦθος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 195e}}; σ. θράσος **stubborn** courage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 261.}}
**III** _adverb_, -ρῶς καθῆσθαι, i.e. **on a hard seat**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 783; εὐνάζεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 12.2.}}
**III.2** **hardly, with difficulty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 282.9.}}
**III.3** **harshly, obstinately**, σ. διαμάχεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 629a}}; ἀπειλεῖν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 885d; τὰ μαλακὰ σ. καὶ τὰ σκληρὰ μαλακῶς λέγειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1408b9; σ. αὐλεῖν [prev. author] “Aud.” 803a20. (Prob. _cognate_ with σκέλλω.)}}

4643:
** σκληρότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**hardness**, opposed to μαλακότης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 523e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 644b14, +others}}
**2** of taste, **harshness**, οἴνου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.14.12.}}
**3** **stiffness, rigidity**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 881a10.}}
**4** ἡ τῆς κοιλίης σ. **costivity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 7.}}
**II** of persons, **harshness, austerity**, τοῦ δαίμονος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.4; σ. καὶ ἀγροικία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 607b, compare 410d; παράδειγμα σκληρότητος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1454b14.}}

4645:
** σκληρ-ύνω**,
**harden**, opposed to μαλάσσω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 45, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 548b23}}:—_passive_, with _perfect_ ἐσκλήρυσμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ὑγρῶν χρήσιος” 6, and -υμμαι}}, **to be hardened, grow hard**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “VM” 22, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 11.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, σ. τὴν καρδίαν τινός **harden** his heart, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.7.3, +others, NT.Heb.3.8,cf. NT.Rom.9.18}}:—_passive_, {{LXX|LXX.Sir.30.12.}}

4646:
** σκολιός**, ά, όν,
**curved, bent** (opposed to ὀρθός, εὐθύς), σ. σίδηρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.86; σ. σκίπωνι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 65 (_anapaest meter_)}}; of rivers and paths, **winding**, ποταμός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.185, compare 2.29; Μαίανδρος σ. εἰς ὑπερβολήν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 12.8.15}}; οἶμος, ἀτραπός, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1541, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 478, etc.; ῥηγμῖνες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 367b14; λαβύρινθος 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 311; πλέγμα ἕλικος “Anthologia Graeca” 7.24 (4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus); πλοκαμῖδες 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 14.182}}; **twisted, tangled**, βάτος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.315 (4th-3rd c.BC: Zenodotus Grammaticus or 3rd c.BC: Rhianus Epicus), compare 11.33 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}; ἐς τὸ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 37.}}
**2** **bent sideways**, δουλείη κεφαλή, σκολιή {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 536; γένυες 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 203}}; ἵππος σ. **crooked made** or **going askew**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 253d.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **crooked**, i.e. **unjust, unrighteous**, θέμιστες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.387}}; μῦθοι, δίκαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 194, 221}}; αἰ σκολιὰν (i.e. ῥήτραν) ὁ δᾶμος ἕλοιτο, Spartan law cited in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 6; λόγος 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1147; ἀπάται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 213; πατέων ὁδοῖς σκολιαῖς [prev. author] “P.” 2.85}}; **riddling, obscure**, ῥημάτια {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 16; τὸ σ. τῆς εἰσόδου (into true science) 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 250.23: rarely of men, ἰθύνει σκολιόν}} makes **the crooked one** straight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 7; σ. καὶ φοβερός 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.551f: with Verbs, σκολιὰ φρονεῖν, opposed to εὐθὺς ἔμμεν, “Scol.” 16; σ. πράττειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173a}}; τυφλὰ καὶ σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 506c, compare “Grg.” 525a}}; σκολιά, τά, **indirect methods**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 13.39.2}}. _adverb_ σκολιῶς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 258, 262; σ. ἔχοντος τοῦ χρησμοῦ 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.91; εἰς πλάγια καὶ σκολιά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 194b.}}
**III** σκολιόν, τό, **intestine**, σπλάγχανα καὶ νεφρὸν καὶ σκολιόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 1002.5 (Milet., 5th-4th c.BC),compare “Schwyzer” 721.23 (Mycale, 4th c.BC), +others}}

4647:
** σκόλοψ**, οπος, ὁ,
**anything pointed**: especially **pale, stake**, κεφαλὴν πῆξαι ἀνὰ σκολόπεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.177; for impaling, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1430, “El.” 898; ἐπὶ σκόλοψι ἀναρτᾶσθαι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 33.15: _plural_ σκόλοπες}}, **palisade**, τείχεα.. σκολόπεσσιν ἀρηρότα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.45; frequently in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, ἐν δὲ [τάφρῳ] σκόλοπας κατέπηξαν 7.441; διά τε σκόλοπας καὶ τάφρον ἔβησαν 8.343, compare 12.63, 15.344; σκόλοπας περὶ τὸ ἕρκος κατέπηξαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.97, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 116, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.5 (_Attic dialect_ usually σταύρωμα).}}
**2** **thorn,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 42(1).121.92 (Epid., 4th c.BC), LXX.Num.33.55, +others, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.49, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 122; σκόλοπες φοίνικος “PMag.Osl.” 1.270, +others, cf. NT.2Cor.12.7.}}
**3** **an instrument for operating on the urethra**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 50.9.4.}}
**4** **point of a fishing-hook**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 3.}}
**II** **tree**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 983 (Lyric poetry).}}

4648:
** σκοπ-έω**,
used by early writers only in _present_ and _imperfect_ _active_ and _middle_ (see. below 11), the other tenses being supplied by σκέπτομαι (which see):—but in later writers we find _future_ σκοπήσω, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Anonymus “Prog.” in Rhetores Graeci 1.615 W., 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 3.10 (_falsa lectio_), (ἐπι-) 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 103.8, (κατα-) 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.4}}: _aorist_ ἐσκόπησα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 1 (προ-), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus “Fragmenta” 54 (assuming variant) (περι-), 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 12.28, etc.: and of}}_middle_, _aorist_ ἐσκοπησάμην (περι-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.32:}}_perfect_ ἐσκόπημαι (προαν-) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.5.6: (compare σκέπτομαι)}}:—**behold, contemplate** (rather of particulars than of universals, of which θεωρέω is more commonly used, but οἱ τὸν ἥλιον ἐκλείποντα θεωροῦντες καὶ σκοπούμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 99d), ἄστρον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.5; πλοῦν μὴ 'ξ ἀπόπτου μᾶλλον ἢ 'γγύθεν σκοπεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 467, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 490; τὰ πόρρω [prev. author] “Rh.” 482; τὰ ἔμπροσθεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.14(17)}}; **examine, inspect**, καταθεῖναί τι.. σκοπεῖν τῷ βουλομένῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12(5).480 (Athenian law, 5th c.BC)}}; σ. παραγραφάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PLips.” 38 ii 2 (4th c.AD): _absolutely_, ἄλλοσε σ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1474}}; σκοπεῖτε **look out, watch**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 232, etc.}}: followed by a clause, σ. ὅπου.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 16; σ. ποῦ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.1, etc.}}: followed by a _preposition_, σ. εἰς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 812.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 305b.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **look to** or **into, consider, examine**, τὰ ἑωυτοῦ σ. **look to** one's own affairs, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8; τὸ σεαυτοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 232d; τὸ ὑμέτερον 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.8; καιρόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.23; τὸ συμφέρον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 342b {σκοπή}; τὸ πρὸς ποσί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 130}}]; τοὺς νόμους πρὸς τοὺς τῇδε with reference to the laws here, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 24a; τι πρὸς ἐμαυτόν [prev. author] “Euthphr.” 9c: _absolutely_, σκοπῶν εὕρισκον ἴασιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 68, compare “Ph.” 282}}: followed by an _accusative_ and _interrogative_ clause, or μή.., σ. τὴν τελευτὴν κῇ ἀποβήσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 506, “OT” 407}}: followed by an _interrogative_ clause alone, σ. πόθεν χρὴ ἄρξασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.8; σ. εἰ.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 41, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 862a (_middle_)}}; ὅπως.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.26}}: sometimes with _genitive_ _person_ as well as _accusative_ or clause, σκόπει δὴ τόδε αὐτῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 182a; πρῶτον αὐτῶν ἐσκόπει πότερα.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.12}}: followed by a _preposition_, ἐξ ὧν ἀγγέλλουσι σκοποῦντες λογιεῖσθε τὰ εἰκότα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.36, compare 1.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.13; πρὸς τὸ ἄρχειν σκοπῶν λογίζομαι [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.6.8; σ. τὰ λοιπὰ πρὸς ὑμᾶς αὐτούς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.31; ἀνομολογούμενοι πρὸς ἀλλήλους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 348b; τόδε περὶ αὐτοῦ [prev. work] 351b, etc.}}; τὴν ὀρθολογίαν περί τι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 239b: with}} _adverb_, _absolutely_, ὀρθῶς σ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 155; καιρίως [prev. author] “Rh.” 339; ἄμεινον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 219a.}}
**3** **look out for**, παῦλαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.32; τι ἀγαθόν [prev. author] “Hier.” 9.10; νεώσοικον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 96; ἐσκόπει γυναῖκά μοι 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.18, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 2.11; σ. ὄνομα κάλλιον αὐτῇ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.991f.}}
**II** _middle_, used like _active_ 1.1 (perhaps implying a more deliberate consideration), with _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 68, “Hel.” 1537; τένοντ᾽ ἐς ὀρθὸν ὄμμασι σκοπουμένη [prev. author] “Med.” 1166.}}
**II.2** ={{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.2, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 964; σ. τύχας βροτῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 262: followed by _relative_, σ. τίνι τρόπῳ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 176b, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48: περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 353a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.10: _absolutely_, ἔνεστι τοῖσιν εὖ σκοπουμένοις ταρβεῖν.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 296.}}
**II.3** ={{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.3, ὅτανπερ ἀδικεῖν ἐπιχειρῶσιν, ἅμα καὶ τὴν ἀπολογίαν σκοποῦνται 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 21.17.}}
**III** rarely in _passive_, σκοπῶν καὶ σκοπούμενος ὑπ᾽ ἄλλων **considering and being considered**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 772d}}; ὁ λόγος.. αἰσχρὸς τοῖς σκοπουμένοις is disgraceful **in the very matter considered**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.54 (assuming variant, τοῖς σ. secl. Dobree).}}

4649:
** σκοπ-ός**, ὁ
(also ἡ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.396, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 66)}}: (σκέπτομαι):— **one that watches, one that looks about** or **after** things, παρὰ δὲ σκοπὸν εἷσεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.359}}; γυναικῶν δμῳάων σ. ἐσσι, of a housekeeper, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” [same place]: in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}}, of gods and kings, with _genitive_ loci, **guardian, protector**, Ὀλύμπου σ. {{Refs|“O.” 1.54; Δάλου 6.59}}; Μαγνήτων σ., of Peleus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“N.” 5.27; τὸν ὑψόθεν σ., φύλακα βροτῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 381 (Lyric poetry); also σκοποὶ τῶν εἰρημένων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 215.}}
**b** **one who watches** or **looks out** to take advantage, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.156}}; **watchful, jealous master**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 945.}}
**2** mostly, **lookout-man, watcher**, stationed in some high place (σκοπιά) to overlook a country, especially in war, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.792, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.365, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.1, 4.1.1, etc.; hence Ἠέλιον.. θεῶν σ. ἠδὲ καὶ ἀνδρῶν “hymnus ad Cererem” 62}}: also, **game-watcher**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.40.}}
**3** **spy, scout**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.324, 526, 561 (later κατάσκοπος) σ. καὶ κατοπτῆρας στρατοῦ ἔπεμψα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 36, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 956}}; of **a messenger who has been sent to learn tidings**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 35, compare “Ph.” 125; σκοπός, ναῶν κατόπτας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 557 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **mark** or **object on which one fixes the eye**, σκοπὸν ἄλλον, ὃν οὔ πώ τις βάλεν ἀνήρ, εἴσομαι αἴ κε τύχωμι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.6}}; ἀπὸ σκοποῦ away from **the mark**, {{Refs|11.344}}; ἀπὸ σ. εἰρηκέναι, εἰρῆσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 179c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.10; παρὰ σκοπόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.94}}; σκοπῷ ἐπέχειν τόξον to aim at **it**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 2.89; σκοποῦ ἄντα τυχεῖν [prev. author] “N.” 6.27; ἔκυρσας ὥστε τοξότης.. σκοποῦ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 628; ὥστε τοξόται σκοποῦ, τοξεύετ᾽ ἀνδρὸς τοῦδε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1033; ἄθλιον σκοπὸν ἐμοὶ ἀκοντίσας 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.6; ἐπὶ σκοπὸν βάλλειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.29; παραλλάξαι τοῦ σ. καὶ ἁμαρτεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 194a; ἀποτυγχάνω τοῦ σκοποῦ [prev. author] “Lg.” 744a.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **aim, end, object**, οὗτος.. δοκεῖ ὁ σ. εἶναι πρὸς ὃν βλέποντα δεῖ ζῆν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 507d; τὴν ἡδονὴν σ. ὀρθὸν πᾶσι ζῴοις γεγονέναι [prev. author] “Phlb.” 60a; στοχάζεσθαι σκοποῦ [prev. author] “R.” 519c; σ. τυραννικὸς τὸ ἡδύ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1311a4, etc.}}; σκοπός.. **nihil praebere** 'his little game' is to make no allowance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 15.29.2, compare argument 11 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Eq.”}}
**II.2.b** in Medicine texts, of healing, ἐπὶ τῷ πρώτῳ σ. by first **intention** (i.e. direct union), κατὰ δεύτερον σ. by second **intention** (i.e. granulation or scar tissue), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.387, compare 10.162.}}
**II.3** **contest in shooting at a mark**, σ. ἱππέων, πεζῶν, {{Refs|“IG” 9(2).527.16,18 (from Larissa).}}
**III** name of **a dance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 446.}}

4650:
** σκορπ-ίζω**,
_future_ **-ιῶ**{{LXX|LXX.Job.39.15}}:— **scatter, disperse**, _Ionic dialect_ word, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 366J. (_passive_)}}; elsewhere only in later writers, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.2Ki.22.15, +others, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 4.4.6, NT.Matt.12.30, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.134, 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 12.2; ἐσκορπισμέναι μύξαι “CPHerm.” 7ii 18 (3rd c.AD).}}
**2** **disintegrate, reduce to powder**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Zos.Alch. p.177 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**3** **dissipate**, τὸν πατρικὸν βίον {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 2.162.}}

4651:
** σκορπί-ος**, ὁ,
**scorpion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 169, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 290a, “Sammelb.” 1267.7 (1st c.AD), etc.}}; σ. ὁ χερσαῖος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(see. below 11) 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 555a23: proverbial, ὑπὸ παντὶ λίθῳ σκορπίον φυλάσσεο 5th c.BC: Praxilla Lyrica 4; ἐν παντὶ σ. φρουρεῖ λίθῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 37}}; also σκορπίον ὀκτώπουν ἐγείρεις 'let sleeping dogs lie', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; ὥσπερ ἔχις ἢ σ. ἠρκὼς τὸ κέντρον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.52.}}
**II** a sea-fish, probably **Scorpaena scrofa**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 261.9, Diocl.fragment 135, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 508b17, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.977f}}; used (like the **mugilis** in Catull.15.19, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Juvenalis, D. Junius Poeta Latinus 10.317) to punish adulterers, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 173.21; uncertain meaning in LXX.1Ki.12.11.}}
**III** **scorpion furze, Genista acanthoclada**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.1.3, 6.4.1.}}
**III.2** **scorpion root, Doronicum caucasicum**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 9.13.6.}}
**III.3** ={θηλυφόνον}, {{Refs|[prev. work] 9.18.2.}}
**IV** the constellation {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Scorpio”, 4th-3rd c.BC(?): Cleostratus Poeta Philosophus 1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 85, 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.2.20, 3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus “Καταστερισμοί” 7.}}
**V** an engine of war for discharging arrows, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 74.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marcellus” 15; σκορπίων σωλῆνες “IG” 22.1627.333.}}
**VI** a stone, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 500, compare 494.}}

4652:
** σκοτειν-ός**, ή, όν,
(σκότος) **dark**, νυκτὸς ἅρμ᾽ ἐπείγεται σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 661; σ. τῶν ἐνερτέρων βέλος [prev. work] 285}}; σ. περιβολαί, of a scabbard, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 276}}; [ὁδοί] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.5; τὰ σ. θεάσασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 520c}}; ἀνὰ τὸ σ. προϊδεῖν in **the darkness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.22}}; of a person, **blind**, καίπερ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1326; σ. ὄμμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 385}}; τὰ σ. the **dark shadows** in a picture, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.57c: _neuter_ as}} _adverb_, σκοτεινὸν ζῆν to live **in privacy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 781c.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **dark, obscure**, opposed to ἐλλόγιμος καὶ φανός, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 197a; τόπος σ. καὶ δυσδιερεύνητος [prev. author] “R.” 432c}}; Heraclitus was called ὁ σκοτεινός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 396b20, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “de Finibus” 2.5.15; σ. προοίμιον 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.34}}; σ. ἀκοαί **obscure** reports, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 109e}}; σ. μηχανήματα **dark, secret**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 288; ὁρκάναι [prev. author] “Ba.” 611; σκοτεινὸς ὀργήν “Tragica Adespota” 345, compare 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 1}}. _adverb_, -νῶς διαλέγεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 558d, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 32.}}
**III** probably _falsa lectio_ for{κοτ-} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.61.}}

4653:
** σκοτί-α**, ἡ,
(σκότος) **darkness, gloom**, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1698, LXX (LXX.Mic.3.6, +others), “NT” (NT.Matt.10.27, +others), cf. Moer. p.354 P.}}
**II** in Architecture, **scotia, cavetto**, a sunken moulding, so called from **the dark shadow** it casts, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 3.5.2, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**III** **Σκοτιά**, epithet of Aphrodite in Egypt, {{Refs|[prev. author]}}

4655:
** σκότος**, ὁ,
more rarely **σκότος**, εος, τό (see. entry near the end), **darkness, gloom**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.389, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 121.4, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 142, etc.}}; opposed to φάος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 319 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 417, etc.}}; opposed to ἡμέρα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 411b.}}
**2** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”}} always of the **darkness** of death, mostly in phrase τὸν δὲ σ. ὄσσε κάλυψεν {{Refs|4.461, +others; στυγερὸς δ᾽ ἄρα μιν σ. εἷλεν 5.47, 13.672}}; so in Trag. and Comedy texts, σκότῳ θανεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 837 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἤδη με περιβάλλει σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1453; σ. γίγνεται 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 40}}; σκότον εἶναι τεθνηκότος (i.e. Αἰσχύλου) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 643.}}
**3** of the nether world, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 130; σκότον νέμονται Τάρταρόν τε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 72, compare “Pers.” 223; τὸν ἀεὶ κατὰ γᾶς σ. εἱμένος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1701 (Lyric poetry)}}; παῖδες ἀρχαίου Σκότου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 106; ἰὼ σ., ἐμὸν φάος [prev. author] “Aj.” 394 (Lyric poetry); γῆς σκότῳ κέκρυπται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 62; σκότου πύλαι [prev. author] “Hec.” 1.}}
**4** the **darkness** of the womb, φυγόντα μητρόθεν σκότον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 664: _plural_, ἐν σκότοισι νηδύος τεθραμμένη [prev. author] “Eu.” 665.}}
**5** of blindness, σκότου νέφος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1313 (Lyric poetry); ὁθούνεκ᾽.. ἐν σκότῳ.. ὀψοίατο, i.e. οὐκέτι ὀψοίατο, [prev. work] 1273}}; βλέποντα νῦν μὲν ὄρθ᾽, ἔπειτα δὲ σκότον, i.e. μηδέν,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 419; σκότον δεδορκώς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 377, compare “HF” 563.}}
**5.b** **dizziness, vertigo**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.23; σκότοι πρὸ τῶν ὀμμάτων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 584a3}}; compare σκοτόδινος, -δινιάω.
**6** _metaphorically_, σκότῳ κρύπτειν hide in **darkness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1396 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Frr.” 42.5, 228}}; σκότον ἔχειν to be in **darkness, obscurity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “N.” 7.13, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1052.8}}; ἀπορία καὶ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 837a; περικαλύψαι τοῖσι πράγμασι σκότον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1522: with Preps., διὰ σκότους ἡ ὁδός}} it is dark and uncertain, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.9; ἐν σ. καθήμενος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.83; μηδὲν ἐν σ. τεχνωμένων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 494; κατὰ σκότον [prev. author] “Ph.” 578; ὑπὸ σκότου [prev. author] “Ant.” 692, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1457 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.4; ὑπὸ σκότῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1030 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1214.}}
**7** of a person, Μητρότιμος ὁ σ., like{ὁ σκοτεινός}, the **mystery-man**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 78}}; also, **darkness**, i.e. **ignorance**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.226}}; **deceit**, σ. καὶ ἀπάτη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 864c.}}
**8** _plural_, σκότη **shadows** in a picture, [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Grammaticus “fragment” 300, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see entry {ἀπεσκοτωμένα}, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 953.51.— 2nd c.AD: Aelius Dionysius Grammaticus “fragment” 217}}] regarded the _masculine_ as the _Attic dialect_ form: the _neuter_ never occurs in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus}}, and is nowhere required by the metre in Trag., though it sometimes occurs in codices, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 831, “HF” 1159, “fragment” 534, variant in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 40, uncertain reading in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 6}}; it is found, however, without variant, in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 42.5}} and _Attic dialect_ Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 516e, “Cra.” 418c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.159, etc.; also in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. έ, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.9, 7.4.18}}; the word is always _neuter_ in LXX and “NT.”
** σκοτί-α**, ἡ,
(σκότος) **darkness, gloom**, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1698, LXX (LXX.Mic.3.6, +others), “NT” (NT.Matt.10.27, +others), cf. Moer. p.354 P.}}
**II** in Architecture, **scotia, cavetto**, a sunken moulding, so called from **the dark shadow** it casts, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 3.5.2, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**III** **Σκοτιά**, epithet of Aphrodite in Egypt, {{Refs|[prev. author]}}

4657:
** σκῠβᾰλ-ον**, τό,
**dung, excrement**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.352d, 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Problemata - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 1.18: _plural_, σ. λευκὰ καὶ ἀργιλώδεα 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.15, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.1.37, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.13.7, etc.}}; **manure,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PFay. 119.7 (1st-2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **refuse, offal,** {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Phil.3.8, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 5.179c}}; ἀποδειπνίδιον σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.302 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}; ἄνδρα, πολύκλαυτον ναυτιλίης σ{{Refs|. [prev. work] 7.276 (Hegesippus Epigrammaticus)}}; τέφρης λοιπὸν ἔτι σ{{Refs 1st c.AD+|. [prev. work] 382 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}; opposed to τὸ χρήσιμον, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.AD: Athenaeus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 1.2.8; σ. τοῦ σησάμου “PCair.Zen.” 494.16 (3rd c.BC)}}; **ς**. χόρτου {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PSI” 3.184.7 (_plural_, 3rd c.AD): _plural_, δεῖπνον ἀπὸ σκυβάλων “Anthologia Graeca” 6.303 (from Aristo)}}; σ. ἀνθρώπου {{LXX|LXX.Sir.27.4.}}

4658:
** Σκύθ-ης**, ου, ὁ:
_vocative_ Σκύθᾰ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 829, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1112, etc.:— “Scythian”, first in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 55: proverbial, Σκυθῶν ἐρημία}}, of a desert, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 704}}: _metaphorically_, **rude, rough person**, ἐν λόγοις Σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.847f, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 533.13.}}
**2** _adjective_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Scythian”, Σ. ἐς οἷμον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 2; Σ. ὅμιλος [prev. work] 417 (Lyric poetry)}}; σίδηρος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 818 (compare Χάλυψ); κύανος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 55.}}
**II** at Athens, **one of the city police**, which was mainly composed of Scythian slaves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1018, 1026, “Lys.” 451; compare τοξότης 111.}}
**II.2** ={ἱπποτοξότης}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 2.13.}}

4659:
** σκυθρωπ-ός**, όν,
also ή, όν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 3.17.ιδ, 4th c.BC: Ephorus Historicus “fragment” 96J., 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.417c, etc.}}: (σκυθρός, ὤψ):— **of sad** or **angry countenance, sullen**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 271, “Hipp.” 1152; ὄμμα καὶ πρόσωπον [prev. author] “Ph.” 1333; σ. τοῖς ξένοις [prev. author] Alcaeus774; ἐπὶ τοῖς κακοῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.4; opposed to ἱλαρός, φαιδρός, [prev. work] 2.7.12, 3.10.4}}; also of affected gravity, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.68, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.20: τὸ σ.,={σκυθρωπότης}, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 797, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 206d}}]. _adverb_, -πῶς ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.1: _comparative_ -ότερον}} **with greater severity**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.2.7.}}
**II** of things, **gloomy, sad, melancholy**, γῆρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1252; ὁδός 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.105 (_comparative_)}}; μέλη {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.7.5; ἡμέρα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 30 (_superlative_).}}
**II.2** of colour, **sad-coloured, dark and dull**, of the river Μέλας, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 23.22}}; of wine,{{Refs|[prev. work] 9.4.}}

4660:
** σκύλλω**,
{{NT|NT.Mark.5.35}}, _aorist_ ἔσκῡλα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus (see. below)}}:—_passive_, see below; _aorist_ ἐσκύλθην {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 769.41, 1516.57}}; also ἐσκύλην [ῠ] (see. below): _perfect_ ἔσκυλμαι (see. below):—= τοῖς ὄνυξι σπᾶν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}_aorist_ _infinitive_ σκοῦλαι (perhaps _Laconian dialect_),= κνῆσαι{{Refs|[prev. author]:}} —_passive_, σκύλλονται, of dead bodies **torn** by fish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 577 (Lyric poetry); ἔσκυλται.. κίκιννος}}**is dishevelled,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 5.174 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus); ἔσκυλται δὲ κόμη [prev. work] 258 (Paul. Sil.).}}
**2** **maltreat, molest**, τοὺς ἐν [τοῖς ἱεροῖς] ἀποτεταγμένους {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 6236.22 (1st c.BC)}}; ὃς δὲ ἂν σκύλῃ [τὸ μνῆμα] {{Refs|“IG” 14.1901 (from Rome), compare “Anthologia Graeca” 3.6 (Inscription Cyzic.), “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3757 (from Nicaea), 4077 (from Ancyra)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 107.8, 16 (2nd c.BC).}}
**3** **trouble, annoy**, τὴν ἀσθενοῦσαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.11; σκύλας καὶ ὑβρίσας 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.3.4; σ. τὸν στρατόν [prev. author] 4.13.3}}; τί σκύλλεις τὸν διδάσκαλο; {{NT|NT.Mark.5.35, cf. NT.Luke.8.49}}; σκῦλον σεαυτὸν πρὸς ἡμᾶς φέρων.. τὴν ὕαλον **bestir** yourself (i.e. **hurry**) to us with.., {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PFay.” 134.2 (4th c.AD):}} —_passive_ and _middle_, μὴ σκύλλου **trouble** not **thyself,** {{NT|NT.Luke.7.6}}; σκυλῆναι πρὸς Τιμόθεον **take the trouble to go** to T., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 123.10 (3rd-4th c.AD)}}; σκυλῆναι ἀνέξετα; will he **trouble to come?** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Phoebammon Rhetor “de Figuris - Rhet.” p.44S.}}; σκῦλαι (_imperative_ _middle_) σεαυτὸν καὶ κτλ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PBaden” 33.6 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἐσκυλμένοι {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Matt.9.36; σκύλλεται καὶ καταπονεῖται 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 1.}}
**II** _middle_, σκύλαιο κάρη **shave** the patient's head, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 410.}}

4662:
** σκωληκό-βρωτος**, ον,
**worm-eaten**, of a tree,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work = 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum”] 3.12.6, “CP” 5.9.1; γῆ “PTeb.” 701.81 (3rd c.BC), “PSI” 5.490.14 (3rd c.BC).}}
**2** **eaten of worms**, of a man, {{NT|NT.Act.12.23.}}

4663:
** σκώληξ**, ηκος, ὁ,
**worm**, especially **earthworm**, ὥς τε σκώληξ ἐπὶ γαίῃ κεῖτο ταθείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.654.}}
**2** _plural_, **grubs** or **larvae** of insects, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1111, “fragment” 583, 5th-6th c.BC: Nicopho Comicus 1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.10.4}}; ἐξ οὗ ὅλου ὅλον γίνεται τὸ ζῷον, opposed to the egg (ᾠόν), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 489b8, compare “GA” 733a1, “HA” 506a26, 551b2, +others}}
**3** _plural_, **worms in dung, in decayed matter, in trees and wood**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.12.6, 5.4.4, etc.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, οἱ κόλακές εἰσι.. οὐσίας σκώληκες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 33.1.}}
**5** **aerugo vermicularis**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.79, 1st c.AD: Andromachus Poeta Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.806.}}
**II** **thread twisted** from the distaff, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Epigenes Comicus 7.}}
**III** _Aeolic dialect_ for κολόκυμα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 25, compare 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.108 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}
**IV** **worm-shaped cake**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus “fragment” 6.}}
**V** **heap of threshed corn**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

4664:
** σμᾰράγδ-ῐνος**, η, ον,
**of smaragdus**, [λίθος] {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Rev.4.3, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.101c.}}
**II** **smaragdusgreen**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Celsus Medicus 5.19.4, “CPR” 27.8 (2nd c.AD)}}; written ζμ- in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PHamb.” 10.25 (2nd c.AD).}}

4665:
** σμάραγδος** [μᾰ], ἡ
(ὁ, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.4.20, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 614)}}, name of several green stones, including the **emerald**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.44, 3.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 110d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 23, +others, LXX.Exo.28.9, +others, Str.[same place], 17.1.45, 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 37.62, +others, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.30, Olymp.Hist. p.466 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, “PMag.Lond.” 46.239}}; also **μάραγδος**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 373, Comedy texts in “PSI” 2.143.3, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica [same place], 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 5.178, 18.80; σφραγὶς μαράγδου “IG” 11(2).161 B 44 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), 199 B 59 ([prev. passage]), but σφραγὶς σμαράγδου 203 B 87 ([prev. passage], 3rd c.BC)}}; **ζμάραγδος** implied in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Jud.Voc.” 9.}}
**II** Σμάραγδος, ἡ, name of the emerald mines in Egypt, ἀρχιμεταλλάρχου τῆς Ζμαράγδου {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Proc.Soc.Bibl. Arch.” 31 (1909). 323 (1st c.AD)}}; μεταλλάρχη (_genitive_ _singular_) Ζμαράκτου {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 660.2 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}; also Σμάραγδος ὄρος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Geographia” 4.5.8.}}

4666:
** σμύρνα**,
frequently written **ζμύρνα** as in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.328.2 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 35.4 (2nd c.BC), “PMag.Par.” 1.781, etc., ἡ,= μύρρα}}, **myrrh**, the gum of an Arabian tree, **Balsamodendron Myrrha** (itself called σμύρνα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 3.14.4, 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 34.5)}}, used for embalming the dead, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.40, 86, compare 73, NT.John.19.39}}; called σμύρνης ἱδρώς by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1175}}; burnt as incense, βωμὸς ἀτμίζων πυρὶ σμύρνης σταλαγμούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 370}}; ὑποθυμιῆν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 6}}; used as an unguent or salve, σμύρνῃ κατάλειπτος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1332; σμύρνῃ ἰώμενοι τὰ ἕλκεα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.181; compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.1.2, 9.4.3 and 10, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.64, etc.}}
**II** **Indian bdellium, Balsamodendron Mukul**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 6.22.4. (The originally form must have been μύρρα, from 3rd c.BC: Phoenix Iambographus}} **môrâh**; compare κιννάμωμον.)

4669:
** σμυρν-ίζω**,
**flavour** or **drug with myrrh**, ἐσμυρνισμένος οἶνος {{NT|NT.Mark.15.23.}}
**2** _intransitive_, **to be like myrrh**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.66.}}

4671:
** σύ** [ῠ],
**thou**: _pronoun_ of the second _person_:—_Epic dialect_ _nominative_ **τύνη** [ῡ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.485, +others}} (_Laconian dialect_ **τούνη** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **σύ** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 16.6, 21.9}}; _Doric dialect_ **τύ** [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.85, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 34, +others, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.33, etc.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **τού** [short syllable] {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.83, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}} (also τούν {{Refs|[prev. work] 50.27, 55.6): Nom.}}**σύ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.31, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1035, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 29, etc.; _vocative_, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.193, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 165, “Pl.” 1069.}}—Gen. **σοῦ**, {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 29.4}}, elsewhere only _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 302, etc.}}; _enclitic_ σου, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 761, “OT” 538, etc.; never in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, who uses σεῦ, lines 3.206, +others, σέο [prev. work] 446, +others}} (also in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus (?)in “PLit.Lond.” 54, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.65), σεῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.137, +others; also σέθεν 1.180, +others}} (which also occurs in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 33, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.9, and Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 264, +others)}}, and as _enclitic_ σευ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.811, +others, σεο 1.396: 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus has only σέο 1.124}}, σεο (_enclitic_) {{Refs|[prev. work] 9, σεῦ [prev. passage], 3.42, 85, 7.38}} σευ (_enclitic_) {{Refs|3.36, 134, 7.49}}:—_Doric dialect_ τεῦ, τευ, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.19, 10.36, etc.}}; rarely τέο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 17; lengthened τεοῦ 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 145, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 84, and τεοῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.37, 468}}; _Boeotian dialect_ τεῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 24}}; _Doric dialect_ τεοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59; also τιοῦς 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.27; τεῦς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.126}}; τοι variant in {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.25}}; _enclitic_ τεος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 83; Cretan dialect τέορ 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; other _Doric dialect_ forms are τίω, τίως, both {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13, τίος 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 75.24.}}—Dat. **σοί**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.158, 167, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 88, 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 8, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 7,99, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 3, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, 6.86.ά, 7.52}}, etc.; _Doric dialect_ τοί {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 86 (oxytone)}}; _Doric dialect_, Lesb., and _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ τοι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|Alem. 33, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Frr.” 2.2,8, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 79, 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 20, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 44, 75.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.76, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.104, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.115, 3.35, 63, 85; in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, Lesbian Lyric poetry, and _Ionic dialect_ Lyric poetry and Prose τοι is always _enclitic_, σοί never _enclitic_ (τοί and σοι are not found except σοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.359, 11.381, ς (οι) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170}}, and in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.270, 9.55}}; rarer than τοι in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, 3.69, +others)}}; in _Attic dialect_ both σοί and σοι (_enclitic_) are used (σοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 361, etc., σοι [prev. work] 87, etc.)}}, τοί and τοι are not used; σοι is never elided except in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170, τοι is elided in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.60, 347, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 55, [prev. author] “Oxy.” 1788 “fragment” 15 ii 9, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 28.2}}; _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also **τεΐν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.201, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.619, epigram cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.60, 61, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 930}}; also **τίν** [ῐ], {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 16, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.29, 8.68 (uncertain reading)}}; **τίν** [ῑ], {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “I.” 6(5).4, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.20 (τίν}} before a consonant, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).93); also τεΐ 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 53; τίνη 3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13.}}—Acc. **σέ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.256, +others}}; _enclitic_ σε,{{Refs 7th c.BC+|1.26, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.2, “Supp.” 23.9, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, etc.}}; in late Gr. σέν, {{Refs|“Anatolian Studies” p.76}}; _Doric dialect_ τέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 52, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.48, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.5, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 114}}; τ᾽ variant (codex R) in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 779 (on the accent see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 54.14, 83.4)}}; τρέ (to be read τϝέ) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; or (_enclitic_) τυ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.69 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1225, “Ach.” 730 (uncertain in “Ach.” 779), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.56, 78, etc.:—also τίν 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 4, 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 7.6, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.39, 55, 68.}}
**2** in combination with γε, **σύ γε,** **σέ γε**, etc. (compare ἔγωγε), **thou at least, for thy part**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and _Attic dialect_; _Doric dialect_ τύγε [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [272], 5th c.BC: Timocreon Lyricus 1 (variant{τύ γα}); τύγᾰ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.69, 71}}]; _Boeotian dialect_ **τούγα** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}}: _dative_ **σοί γε** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.557}}: _accusative_ **σέ γε** {{Refs|10.96}}, etc.:—also **σύ περ** {{Refs|24.398.}}
**3** σύ with _infinitive_ (as _imperative_), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, 4.163.}}
**II** Dual _nominative_ and _accusative_ **σφῶϊ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.336, 4.286, +others}}, **you two, both of you**; **σφώ** (not σφῴ,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 85.17), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.574, 11.782, 13.47, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 344, 1543, etc.}}—Gen. and Dat. **σφῶϊν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.341, +others}}; _contraction_ σφῷν once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.62, and always in _Attic dialect_ (Hdn.Gr. 1.475) and Trag., e.g. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 12, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 342, “OT” 1495, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 892e (codices 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus have σφῶϊν in “Tht.” 193c, +others, σφῶν “Euthd.” 273e, +others)}}. None of these forms are _enclitic_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 38.9, 85.12; codex A of 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 658c, 673e, 689a wrongly makes σφῳν}} _enclitic_; Ζεὺς σφὼ is prescribed in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.146 by Hdn.Gr. 2.93.}} —σφῶϊ is never _dative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.286}} it is the _accusative_ depending on κελεύ; σφῶϊν is never _accusative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.52 it is dative commodi.}}
**III** Plur. _nominative_ **ὑμεῖς**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.75, +others (before a vowel, 4.246, 7.194, +others), 6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus 11, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 29a, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72, etc.}}, **ye, you**; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.274, +others, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 24, 25}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 60, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 760, 761, 862; ὑμέν “SIG” 685.118 (Crete, 2nd c.BC), “GDI” 5155.6 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.BC)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμές {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 6}}; a resolved form ὑμέες, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus “fragment” 14, is a poeticlicence (so 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.3)}} rather than genuine Ionic {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.22).}}— Gen. **ὑμῶν**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 143, etc.}}; ὑμέων (_disyllable_) [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.159, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.7, +others, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74.6 (ὑμῶν codices), 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 11.5 (variant{ὑμῶν})}}]; ὑμέων also {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.73, 6.130, +others; as _trisyllable_, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.27; ὑμείων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.348, 7.195, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 46}}; also ὑμῶν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.23}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ὑμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 96}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμίων {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 22.}}—Dat. **ὑμῖν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.46, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ ὗμῐν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 97.28}} also _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 91}}; _Doric dialect_ (not _enclitic_) ὑμίν [ῐ] {{Refs|[prev. author] 92}}; ὑμίν [ῐ] also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 864, 1242, “OT” 991, 1402, “Ant.” 308, “El.” 804, +others (but ὗμιν}} should perhaps be _restored_ where the sense needs an enclitic on the principle stated by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 35.6, 36.5, “Synt.” 130.23)}}; ὕμιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(as _enclitic_) is prescribed by Hdn.Gr. (2.124) in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.33, by “Etymologicum Magnum” 432.34 in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.376, 2.141, etc.:—_Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμῐ, ὔμμῐν, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.316, 11.336, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 328, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 14, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 26.9, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).17.}}—Acc. **ὑμᾶς**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 325}}, etc. (**-υ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 820, variant in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 222}}; ὗμας or (more probably) ὕμας is required by the metre in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 9.9, 47.11)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ὑμέας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.198, +others}}; _enclitic_ ὕμεας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.60 (Papyrus)}}; ὑμέας also {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, +others}}; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.412, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).19; also in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 620 (trimeter), and 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 846 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 3, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 52, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 737, “Lys.” 1076, “SIG” 528.3 (Cretan dialogue, 3rd c.BC), 622 B 8 (Cretan, 2nd c.BC).}}—The _plural_ is sometimes used in addressing **one** person, when others are included in the speaker's thought, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.81, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 89. (With σύ}} cf. Latin _tu_, Gothic _pu_; with τοι Sanskrit _genitive_ and _dative_ **te**; the origin of σφῶϊ is doubtful; with ὑμεῖς cf. Sanskrit _accusative_ _plural_ **yusmān.**)

4673:
** σορός**, ἡ,
**vessel for holding** human remains, **cinerary urn**, ὣς δὲ καὶ ὀστέα νῶϊν ὁμὴ σ. ἀμφικαλύπτοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.91}}; **coffin**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68, 2.78, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 691, “Lys.” 600, etc.}}; of stone, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 46, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.124: proverbial, τὸν ἕτερον πόδα ἐν τῇ σ. ἔχειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 78}}; **bier,** {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Luke.7.14, “PLond.” 1.121.236 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** as nickname of an old man or woman, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1365, 3rd c.BC: Macho Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.580c.}}
**III** αἱ δημόσιαι σ. uncertain meaning in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLips.” 86.11 (4th c.AD).}}

4674:
** σός**, ή, όν,
possessive _adjective_ of 2 _person_ _singular_ (σύ), the alternative _Epic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ form being τεός (which see), **thy, thine**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.420, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _genitive_ σοῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.511}}; σ. δέμας, σ. ἔργον, λέχος σ., etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 146 (Lyric poetry), 635, 557 (Lyric poetry), etc.: σ. ἑταῖρος}} a friend **of yours**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204a, etc.}}: with the _Article_, τὸ σὸν γέρας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.185, compare 207, +others, and so frequently in _Attic dialect_, δέμ·ας τὸ σ., τὸ σ. κάρα, etc., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1019, “Ag.” 1615, etc.}} (but never so when it serves as predicate, οὐ σ. τόδ᾽ ἐστὶ τοὔργον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 296; πάτερ, σός εἰμι [prev. author] “Ant.” 635)}}; σ. ἔργον with _infinitive_, 'tis **thy** business to.., ἔργον ἤδη σ. τὰ λοίφ᾽ ὑπηρετεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 15}}; σόν [ἐστι] alone, σ. δ᾽ αὖ τὸ σιγᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 232, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1470}}; σὴ μὲν ἐγώ, σὰ δὲ πάντα **thine** am I, **thine** are all things, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 219.}}
**2** without a _substantive_, **thine**, εἰ ἐτεόν γε σός εἰμι **thy son**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.529, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 226 (Lyric poetry)}}; σὺ μὲν ἀπάγου τὴν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.37}}; οἱ σ. **thy kinsfolk, people**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1448, etc.}}: also _singular_, **your agent** or **servant,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PFay. 123.5 (1st-2nd c.AD): τὸ σ}}. **what concerns thee, thy interest, advantage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 251, “Aj.” 1313}}; **thy words, thy purpose**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 99, 1401}}, etc.; τὰ σ. **thy property**, ἐπὶ σοῖσι καθήμενος {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.369, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.12, NT.Luke.6.30}}; εὖ φρονῶ τὰ σ. **thy interests**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 491}}; καὶ σὲ καὶ τὰ σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 522, etc.}}
**3** with a _genitive_ added, τὰ σ᾽ αὐτῆς ἔργα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.490}}; τὰ σ᾽ αὐτοῦ κήδε (α) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.185; σῷ δ᾽ αὐτοῦ κράατι 22.218; τοῖς σοῖσιν αὐτοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 416; τὸ σὸν μόνης δώρημα [prev. author] “Tr.” 775}}; τὸν σ. τοῦ πρέσβεως [ὀφθαλμόν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 93.}}
**II** objective, **of** or **for thee**, σῇ ποθῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.321; σ. τε πόθος σ. τε μήδεα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.202; σῇ προμηθίᾳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 332; προνοίᾳ τῇ τε σ. κἀμῇ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 660}}; εὐνοίᾳ τῇ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 486a.}}

4675:
** σύ** [ῠ],
**thou**: _pronoun_ of the second _person_:—_Epic dialect_ _nominative_ **τύνη** [ῡ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.485, +others}} (_Laconian dialect_ **τούνη** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **σύ** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 16.6, 21.9}}; _Doric dialect_ **τύ** [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.85, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 34, +others, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.33, etc.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **τού** [short syllable] {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.83, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}} (also τούν {{Refs|[prev. work] 50.27, 55.6): Nom.}}**σύ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.31, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1035, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 29, etc.; _vocative_, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.193, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 165, “Pl.” 1069.}}—Gen. **σοῦ**, {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 29.4}}, elsewhere only _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 302, etc.}}; _enclitic_ σου, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 761, “OT” 538, etc.; never in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, who uses σεῦ, lines 3.206, +others, σέο [prev. work] 446, +others}} (also in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus (?)in “PLit.Lond.” 54, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.65), σεῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.137, +others; also σέθεν 1.180, +others}} (which also occurs in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 33, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.9, and Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 264, +others)}}, and as _enclitic_ σευ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.811, +others, σεο 1.396: 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus has only σέο 1.124}}, σεο (_enclitic_) {{Refs|[prev. work] 9, σεῦ [prev. passage], 3.42, 85, 7.38}} σευ (_enclitic_) {{Refs|3.36, 134, 7.49}}:—_Doric dialect_ τεῦ, τευ, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.19, 10.36, etc.}}; rarely τέο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 17; lengthened τεοῦ 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 145, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 84, and τεοῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.37, 468}}; _Boeotian dialect_ τεῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 24}}; _Doric dialect_ τεοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59; also τιοῦς 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.27; τεῦς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.126}}; τοι variant in {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.25}}; _enclitic_ τεος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 83; Cretan dialect τέορ 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; other _Doric dialect_ forms are τίω, τίως, both {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13, τίος 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 75.24.}}—Dat. **σοί**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.158, 167, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 88, 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 8, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 7,99, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 3, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, 6.86.ά, 7.52}}, etc.; _Doric dialect_ τοί {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 86 (oxytone)}}; _Doric dialect_, Lesb., and _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ τοι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|Alem. 33, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Frr.” 2.2,8, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 79, 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 20, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 44, 75.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.76, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.104, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.115, 3.35, 63, 85; in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, Lesbian Lyric poetry, and _Ionic dialect_ Lyric poetry and Prose τοι is always _enclitic_, σοί never _enclitic_ (τοί and σοι are not found except σοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.359, 11.381, ς (οι) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170}}, and in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.270, 9.55}}; rarer than τοι in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, 3.69, +others)}}; in _Attic dialect_ both σοί and σοι (_enclitic_) are used (σοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 361, etc., σοι [prev. work] 87, etc.)}}, τοί and τοι are not used; σοι is never elided except in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170, τοι is elided in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.60, 347, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 55, [prev. author] “Oxy.” 1788 “fragment” 15 ii 9, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 28.2}}; _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also **τεΐν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.201, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.619, epigram cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.60, 61, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 930}}; also **τίν** [ῐ], {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 16, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.29, 8.68 (uncertain reading)}}; **τίν** [ῑ], {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “I.” 6(5).4, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.20 (τίν}} before a consonant, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).93); also τεΐ 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 53; τίνη 3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13.}}—Acc. **σέ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.256, +others}}; _enclitic_ σε,{{Refs 7th c.BC+|1.26, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.2, “Supp.” 23.9, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, etc.}}; in late Gr. σέν, {{Refs|“Anatolian Studies” p.76}}; _Doric dialect_ τέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 52, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.48, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.5, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 114}}; τ᾽ variant (codex R) in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 779 (on the accent see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 54.14, 83.4)}}; τρέ (to be read τϝέ) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; or (_enclitic_) τυ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.69 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1225, “Ach.” 730 (uncertain in “Ach.” 779), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.56, 78, etc.:—also τίν 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 4, 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 7.6, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.39, 55, 68.}}
**2** in combination with γε, **σύ γε,** **σέ γε**, etc. (compare ἔγωγε), **thou at least, for thy part**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and _Attic dialect_; _Doric dialect_ τύγε [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [272], 5th c.BC: Timocreon Lyricus 1 (variant{τύ γα}); τύγᾰ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.69, 71}}]; _Boeotian dialect_ **τούγα** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}}: _dative_ **σοί γε** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.557}}: _accusative_ **σέ γε** {{Refs|10.96}}, etc.:—also **σύ περ** {{Refs|24.398.}}
**3** σύ with _infinitive_ (as _imperative_), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, 4.163.}}
**II** Dual _nominative_ and _accusative_ **σφῶϊ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.336, 4.286, +others}}, **you two, both of you**; **σφώ** (not σφῴ,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 85.17), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.574, 11.782, 13.47, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 344, 1543, etc.}}—Gen. and Dat. **σφῶϊν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.341, +others}}; _contraction_ σφῷν once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.62, and always in _Attic dialect_ (Hdn.Gr. 1.475) and Trag., e.g. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 12, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 342, “OT” 1495, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 892e (codices 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus have σφῶϊν in “Tht.” 193c, +others, σφῶν “Euthd.” 273e, +others)}}. None of these forms are _enclitic_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 38.9, 85.12; codex A of 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 658c, 673e, 689a wrongly makes σφῳν}} _enclitic_; Ζεὺς σφὼ is prescribed in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.146 by Hdn.Gr. 2.93.}} —σφῶϊ is never _dative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.286}} it is the _accusative_ depending on κελεύ; σφῶϊν is never _accusative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.52 it is dative commodi.}}
**III** Plur. _nominative_ **ὑμεῖς**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.75, +others (before a vowel, 4.246, 7.194, +others), 6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus 11, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 29a, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72, etc.}}, **ye, you**; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.274, +others, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 24, 25}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 60, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 760, 761, 862; ὑμέν “SIG” 685.118 (Crete, 2nd c.BC), “GDI” 5155.6 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.BC)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμές {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 6}}; a resolved form ὑμέες, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus “fragment” 14, is a poeticlicence (so 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.3)}} rather than genuine Ionic {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.22).}}— Gen. **ὑμῶν**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 143, etc.}}; ὑμέων (_disyllable_) [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.159, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.7, +others, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74.6 (ὑμῶν codices), 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 11.5 (variant{ὑμῶν})}}]; ὑμέων also {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.73, 6.130, +others; as _trisyllable_, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.27; ὑμείων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.348, 7.195, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 46}}; also ὑμῶν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.23}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ὑμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 96}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμίων {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 22.}}—Dat. **ὑμῖν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.46, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ ὗμῐν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 97.28}} also _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 91}}; _Doric dialect_ (not _enclitic_) ὑμίν [ῐ] {{Refs|[prev. author] 92}}; ὑμίν [ῐ] also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 864, 1242, “OT” 991, 1402, “Ant.” 308, “El.” 804, +others (but ὗμιν}} should perhaps be _restored_ where the sense needs an enclitic on the principle stated by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 35.6, 36.5, “Synt.” 130.23)}}; ὕμιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(as _enclitic_) is prescribed by Hdn.Gr. (2.124) in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.33, by “Etymologicum Magnum” 432.34 in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.376, 2.141, etc.:—_Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμῐ, ὔμμῐν, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.316, 11.336, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 328, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 14, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 26.9, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).17.}}—Acc. **ὑμᾶς**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 325}}, etc. (**-υ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 820, variant in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 222}}; ὗμας or (more probably) ὕμας is required by the metre in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 9.9, 47.11)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ὑμέας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.198, +others}}; _enclitic_ ὕμεας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.60 (Papyrus)}}; ὑμέας also {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, +others}}; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.412, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).19; also in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 620 (trimeter), and 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 846 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 3, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 52, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 737, “Lys.” 1076, “SIG” 528.3 (Cretan dialogue, 3rd c.BC), 622 B 8 (Cretan, 2nd c.BC).}}—The _plural_ is sometimes used in addressing **one** person, when others are included in the speaker's thought, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.81, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 89. (With σύ}} cf. Latin _tu_, Gothic _pu_; with τοι Sanskrit _genitive_ and _dative_ **te**; the origin of σφῶϊ is doubtful; with ὑμεῖς cf. Sanskrit _accusative_ _plural_ **yusmān.**)

4678:
** σοφία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, properly **cleverness** or **skill** in handicraft and art, as in carpentry, τέκτονος, ὅς ῥά τε πάσης εὖ εἰδῇ σ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.412}}; of the Telchines, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.53}}; ἡ ἔντεχνος σ., of Hephaestus and Athena, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 32 1d}}; of Daedalus and Palamedes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.33, compare 1.4.2}}; in music and singing, τέχνῃ καὶ σ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 483, compare 511; in poetry, 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.52, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.117, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 882, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.8, etc.; in driving, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 123c}}; in medicine or surgery, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.54; in divination, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 502 (Lyric poetry); δυσθανατῶν ὑπὸ σοφίας εἰς γῆρας ἀφίκετο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 406b; σ. δημηγορική, δικανική, [prev. work] 365d}}; ἡ περὶ Ὁμήρου σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ion” 542a; οὐ σοφίᾳ ἀλλὰ φύσει ποιεῖν [prev. author] “Ap.” 22b; σημαίνοντες τὴν σ..., ὅτι ἀρετὴ τέχνης ἐστίν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1141a12}}: rare in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.107, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 676 (Lyric poetry), “IG” 12.522 (vase, 5th c.BC).}}
**2** **skill** in matters of common life. **sound judgement, intelligence, practical wisdom**, etc., such as was attributed to the seven sages, like{φρόνησις}, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 790, 876, 1074, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, 60}}; ἡ τῶν δεινῶν σ., opposed to ἀμαθία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 360d}}; τὴν τότε καλουμένην σ., οὖσαν δὲ δεινότητα πολιτικὴν καὶ δραστήριον σύνεσιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 2}}; also, **cunning, shrewdness, craft**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68, etc.}}; τὸ λοιδορῆς αι θεοὺς ἐχθρὰ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.38.}}
**3** **learning, wisdom**, μείζω τινὰ ἢ κατ᾽ ἄνθρωπον σοφίαν σοφοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20e; opposed to ἀμαθία, [prev. work] 22e}}; frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, e.g. μόρσιμα.. οὐ σοφίᾳ τις ἀπώσεται “Heracl.” 615 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὸ σοφὸν οὐ σοφία (see. σοφός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1.3) “Ba.” 395 (Lyric poetry), etc.; frequently in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}, **speculative wisdom, ** “EN” 1141a19, {{Refs|“Metaphorical” 982a2, 995b12 (_plural_), 1059a18}}; defined as θείων τε καὶ ἀνθρωπίνων ἐπιστήμη, {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.15}}; but also of **natural philosophy and mathematics**, σ. τις καὶ ἡ φυσική {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1005b1, compare 1061b33.}}
**4** among the Jews, ἀρχὴ σοφίας φόβος Κυρίου {{LXX|LXX.Pro.1.7, cf. LXX.Job.28.28, +others}}; Σοφία, recognized first as an attribute of God, was later identified with the Spirit of God,{{LXX|cf. LXX.Pro.8 with LXX.Sir.24s q.}}
**5** later as a title, ἡ ὑμετέρα, ἡ ὑμῶν σ., {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1165.6, “PSI” 7.790.14 (both 6th c.AD).}}

4679:
** σοφ-ίζω**,
**make wise, instruct**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.18(19).8; τινὰ εἰς σωτηρίαν NT.2Tim.3.15.}}
**2** _passive_, **become** or **be clever** or **skilled in** a thing, with _genitive_ _of things_, ναυτιλίης σεσοφισμένος **skilled in** seamanship, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 649; Μοίσαι σεσοφισμέναι 6th c.BC: Ibycus Lyricus “POxy.” 1790.23}}; so ἐν τοῖς ὀνόμασι σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 13.6}}: _absolutely_, **to become** or **be wise**, frequently in {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX, LXX.Eccl.7.24(23), +others; βέλτερος ἀλκήεντος ἔφυ σεσοφισμένος ἀνήρ 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 130.}}
**3** _middle_, **teach oneself, learn**, ἐσοφίσατο ὅτι.. he **became aware** that.., {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.3.8.}}
**II** _middle_ **σοφίζομαι**, with _aorist_ _middle_ and _perfect_ _passive_ (see. below), **practise an art**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 19, “IG” 12.678}}; **play subtle tricks, deal subtly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 744, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.227, etc.}}; οὐδὲν σοφιζόμεσθα τοῖσι δαίμοσι **we use** no **subtleties** in dealing with the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 200}}; **to be scientific, speculate**, περὶ τὸ ὄνομα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 509d, compare “Plt.” 299b, 1st c.AD: Musonius Philosophus “fragment” 3p.12H., etc.}}; σοφιζόμενος φάναι to say **rationalistically**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229c}}; καίπερ οὕτω τούτου σεσοφισμένου though he **has dealt** thus **craftily**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.28}}; σοφίσασθαι πρός τι **to use fraud** for an end, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.58.12}}; οἱ ἰητροὶ σοφιζόμενοι ἔστιν οἳ ἁμαρτάνουσι **when they deal in subtleties**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 1}}; οἱ μυθικῶς σοφ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1000a18, compare “HA” 582a35, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.56}}; σ. πρὸς τὸν νόμον **evade** it, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 27.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **devise cleverly** or **skilfully**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.66, 8.27, compare 1.80; καινὰς ἰδέας σοφίζεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 547; χαρίεντα καὶ σοφά [prev. author] “Av.” 1401}}; ἀλλότρια σ. **meddle with** other men's **craft**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 299}}; with internal _accusative_, ἀνόητα σ. **exercise one's skill** without νοῦς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 283a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.46; ὅσα.. σοφίζονται πρὸς τὸν δῆμον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1297a14}}; ἀλλ᾽ αὐτὸ τοῦτο δεῖ σοφισθῆναι this is the very thing one must **gain by craft**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 77}}; οἶνον ἀπὸ τῶν φοινίκων σ. **make spurious** wine, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 2.6; πορφύραν παρὰ τῆς κόχλου [prev. author] “Her.” 19.15:}} —_passive_, σεσοφισμένοι μῦθοι **craftily devised**, {{NT|NT.2Pet.1.16.}}
**II.2.b** σ. νόμον **evade** it, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 2.40, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 2.41, 4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 50, “OGI” 383.208 (Commagene, 1st c.BC).}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ _person_, **deceive**, τὸν Τίτον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.2.3; μή με σοφίζου “Anthologia Graeca” 12.25 (Stat. Flacc.); τὸν δῆμον 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.10.7; also σ. τὴν αἴσθησιν 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.15.}}
**II.4** '**counter**' by a device, σοφίζεται τὴν βίαν τοῦ μηχανήματος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 3.7.20.}}

4680:
** σοφός**, ή, όν,
**skilled in any handicraft** or **art, clever**, ἁρματηλάτας σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.115, compare “N.” 7.17; κυβερνήτης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 770; μάντις [prev. author] “Th.” 382; οἰωνοθέτας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 484 (Lyric poetry)}}; of a sculptor, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 372}}; even of hedgers and ditchers, {{Refs|Margites “fragment” 2}}; but in this sense mostly of poets and musicians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.9, “P.” 1.42, 3.113}}; ἐν κιθάρᾳ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1238 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 896 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; τὴν τέχνην -ώτερος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 766; περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 696c}}; γλώσσῃ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 88.10; σοφὸς ὁ πολλὰ εἰδὼς φυᾷ, μαθόντες δὲ λάβροι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.86.}}
**2** **clever in practical matters, wise, prudent**, ὁ χρήσιμ᾽ εἰδώς, οὐχ ὁ πόλλ᾽ εἰδώς, σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 390}}; especially **statesmanlike**, in which sense the seven Sages were so called, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Dicaearchus Geographus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.40}}: hence, **shrewd, worldly-wise**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 120, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.12, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.85; σ. ἄνδρες εἰσὶ Θεσσαλοὶ [prev. author] 7.130; σ. παλαιστὴς.., ἀλλὰ χαἱ χαἱ σοφαὶ γνῶμαι.. ἐμποδίζονται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 431, compare 440, “Aj.” 1374}}; πολλὰ σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1295}}; ἃ δεῖ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 655f; τῶν λεγομένων πονηρῶν μέν, σοφῶν δέ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 519a: also σοφαὶ πραπίδες 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).10; φύσις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1282}}: even of animals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 3.7 (_comparative_), 6.13 (_superlative_)}}; σ. πειθώ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.39 codices (σοφοῖς Bgk.)}}; εὐβουλία {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1038: τὸ σ}}. my little **trick**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 502d}}; your **clever notion**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Euthd.” 293d}}; τἀπ᾽ ἐμοῦ σοφά, δάκρυα my tears, **all the resources that I have**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1214}}; εἰ δίκαια, τῶν σοφῶν κρείσσω τάδε better **than all craft**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1246}}; σοφόν [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 228.}}
**2.b** more generally, **learned, wise**, τὸ μὲν σ. [αὐτὸν] καλεῖν ἔμοιγε μέγα εἶναι δοκεῖ καὶ θεῷ μόνῳ πρέπειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278d, compare 279c, “Prt.” 329e, “Ap.” 21a (_comparative_), 22c (_superlative_); opposed to ἀμαθής, [prev. work] 25d (_comparative_)}}; of sophists,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 20a, “Prt.” 309d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.21, etc.}}; **universally and ideally wise**, ὁ σ., τουτέστιν ὁ τὴν τοῦ ἀληθοῦς ἐπιστήμην ἔχων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.42, compare 3.167, +others: later σοφώτατος}} as a title, especially of lawyers or professors, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“PIand.” 16.4 (5th-6th c.AD), “POxy.” 126.6 (6th c.AD).}}
**3** **subtle, ingenious**, opposed to ἀμαθής {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(1445) and σαφής, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1434 (_adverb_)}}; σοφόν τοι τὸ σαφές, οὐ τὸ μὴ σαφές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 397}}; τὸ σοφὸν οὐ σοφία **wisdom overmuch** is no wisdom, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 395 (Lyric poetry)}}; τί οὖν ἦν τοῦτ; οὐδὲν ποικίλον οὐδὲ σοφόν nothing curious or **recondite**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.37.}}—For the senses of ς., see {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1141a10.—mostly _absolutely_, but with _accusative_ _of things_, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 655, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 17c, etc.}}; also ἐν οἰωνοῖς, κιθάρᾳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 662, 1238 (Lyric poetry); εἴς τι [prev. author] “fragment” 162 (_superlative_)}}; περί τι or τινος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203a, “Ap.” 19c: rarely with _genitive_, σοφὸς κακῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 453: also with}}_infinitive_, πῶς δῆτ᾽ ἔγωγ᾽ ἂν.. Διὸς γενοίμην εὖ φρονεῖν σοφώτερο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 524.7.}}
**II** of things, **cleverly devised, wise**, νόμος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196 (_superlative_)}}; νοήματα, ἔπεα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.72 (_superlative_), “P.” 4.138, etc.; γνῶμαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1091; νοῦς [prev. author] “El.” 1016}}; πάντα προσφέρων σοφά all **wise sayings**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 763, compare “Ph.” 1245; χρόνου τε διατριβὰς σοφωτάτας ἐφηῦρε [prev. author] “fragment” 479; σοφώτερ᾽ ἢ κατ᾽ ἄνδρα συμβαλεῖν ἔπη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 675; σ. φυγή [prev. author] “Supp.” 151}}; οὐδὲν σοφὸν εἶναι shows no great wisdom, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1137a10.}}
**III** _adverb_ σοφῶς **cleverly, wisely**, etc., first (?) in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus (?) “fragment” 1122; then in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 699, “Ba.” 1271 codices, “Heracl.” 558, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1434, etc.}}: _comparative_ **-ώτερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1007}}: _superlative_ **-ώτατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hel.” 1528, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 522:}} —σοφῶς, as an exclamation of applause, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.45f, 1st c.AD: Martialis Epigrammaticus Latinus 3.46.8, etc.}} (Not in _Epic dialect_, except in Margites [prev. cited] and as ancient {{Refs 8th c.BC+|variant (12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1023.14) in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.712}}; but see at {σοφία},{σοφίζομαι}.)

4682:
** σπᾰράσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **σπαράττω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 688, etc.}}, _future_ **-ξω**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1018}}: _aorist_ ἐσπάραξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 95.40, (κατ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 688, etc.}}, _future_ **-ξομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1209 (in “IA” 1458 σπαράσσεσθαι is restored for σπαράξεσθαι in _passive_ sense):}} —_passive_,_perfect_ ἐσπάρακται (δι-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 15.3}}:—**tear, rend**, especially of dogs, carnivorous animals, and the like, σάρκας ἐσπάρασσ᾽ ἀπ᾽ ὀστέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1217; σ. τὰς γνάθους 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 428:}} —_middle_, σπαράσσεσθαι κόμαν **tear one's** hair, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1209 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** **rend asunder**, φάραγγα βροντῇ.. πατὴρ σπαράξει A. [prev. cited]
**3** _metaphorically_, **pull to pieces, attack**, ἄνδρα σπαράττων καὶ ταράττων καὶ κυκῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 688; σ. τινὰς τῷ λόγῳ ὥσπερ σκυλάκια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 539b; τὰς ἀρχάς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.50, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 641, “PPetr.” 2p.57 (3rd c.BC), 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.57, 3rd c.BC: Teles Philosophus p.19}} H.:—_passive_, λώβαισι.. ἐσπαραγμένους {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 656.}}
**4** in Medicine texts, σ. τὸ στόμα τῆς κοιλίας provoke sickness, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.57}}; compare σπαρακτέον:—_passive_, σ. ἀνημέτως **retch** without being able to vomit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 546.}}
**4.b** **convulse**, of an evil spirit, {{NT|NT.Mark.1.26.}}

4685:
** σπάω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1003, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 498, etc.:}}_future_ σπάσω [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 484, (δια-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.236, (ἐπι-) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 769}}: _aorist_ ἔσπᾰσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.178 (tmesis)}}, _Epic dialect_ σπάσα {{Refs|5.859 (tmesis)}}, etc.: _perfect_ ἔσπᾰκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 930a21, (ἀν-) 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐπικυήσιος” 22, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1069}}:—_middle_, _future_ σπάσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.38, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐσπᾰσάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.387, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.29, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 7.42}}, _Epic dialect_ σπασάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.166, _Epic dialect_ also σπάσσασθε, σπασσάμενος (see. below)}}:—_passive_, _future_ σπασθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.760, (δια-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.10}}: _aorist_ ἐσπάσθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.458, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἔσπασμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 1.20, (δι-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.98, etc.}}; also in _middle_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.16, “Cyr.” 7.5.29. Mostly poetry (ἕλκω being preferred in Prose)}}:—**draw**, hence,
**I** of a sword, **draw**, mostly in _middle_, φάσγανά τε σπάσσασθε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.74; σπασσάμενος.. ἄορ παχέος παρὰ μηροῦ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.473; ἐκ δ᾽ ἄρα σύριγγος.. ἐσπάσατ᾽ ἔγχος 19.387; σπασαμένων τὰς μαχαίρας “PTeb.” 48.19 (2nd c.BC), compare 138 (2nd c.BC)}}: in _active_, ξίφος σπάσαντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1194; φάσγανον σπάσας χερί [prev. author] “IT” 322:}} —_passive_, ἐσπασμένοι τὰ ξίφη **having their** swords **drawn**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.16; ἐσπασμένον ὃν εἶχεν ἀκινάκην [prev. author] “Cyr.” 7.5.29; ἐσπασμένοις τοῖς ξίφεσι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.52.}}
**I.2** of other things, ἐκ χειρὸς χεῖρα σπάσατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.321; σπασάμην ῥῶπάς τε λύγους τε 10.166}}; ὡς ἕκαστος ἔσπασεν τύχης πάλον **drew** the lot (out of the helmet), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 333:}} —_passive_, σπασθέντος (i.e. ἔγχεος ἔξω χροός) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.458.}}
**I.3** _absolutely_, σπᾶτ᾽ ἀνδρείως **pull, hoist away**, like men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 498.}}
**II** of violent actions, **pluck off** or **out**, κόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1243; λάχνην [prev. author] “Tr.” 690}}; compare σπαστέος.
**II.2** **tear, rend**, especially of ravenous animals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 258, 1003}}; λαιμοτόμους κεφαλάς uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 776 (Lyric poetry)}}; σ. τοῖς ὄνυξιν [τοὺς νεοττούς], of the eagle, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 619b31:}} —_passive_, φλέβιον, σάρκα σπασθῆναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 1.17.}}
**II.3** **wrench, sprain**, τὸ σκέλος ἔσπασε {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 33}}:—_passive_, τὸν μηρὸν σπασθῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.134; τοὺς πόδας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 639.}}
**II.4** **snatch, tear** or **drag away**, πῶλον παρὰ συννόμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 666e; ὑπὸ πτερῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 441}}:—_passive_, ἔλαφον ἀπ᾽ ἐμῶν γονάτων σπασθεῖσαν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 92 (Lyric poetry); ἐκ βραχίονος σπασθείς [prev. work] 408.}}
**II.5** _metaphorically_, **carry away, draw aside**, ἀλλά σ᾽ ἔσπασεν πειθώ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 561; τὰ πάθη οἷον νεῦρα σ. ἡμᾶς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 644e.}}
**II.6** in Medicine texts, **cause convulsion** or **spasm**, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 67}}:—_passive_, **to be convulsed**, σπασθεὶς ἀποθνῄσκει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aph.” 5.5, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.4.13, etc.}}; ἐσπᾶτο γὰρ πέδονδε καὶ μετάρσιος, of Heracles in his agony, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 786, compare σπάσμα, σπασμός}}: _metaphorically_, **to be harassed, anxious**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.1.16.}}
**III** **draw in, suck in**, θρόμβον αἵματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 533; ἔσπασεν ἄμυστιν ἑλκύσας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 417}}; συνεκθανεῖν σπῶντα χρὴ τῷ πώματι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 571; μεστὴν ἀκράτου Θηρίκλειον ἔσπασεν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 5, compare 285}}; opposed to λάπτω, κάπτω, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.699d, compare σπάσις 11}}; σ. τὸν μαστόν **suck** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 587a33; σ. ἀμυστί 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 6.51}}; and in _middle_, ταυρείου σπασάμενος αἵματος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 1.9.27}}:—_passive_, of the female, **to be sucked**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 576b11 (τὸ ἄγαν σπᾶσθαι probable reading);compare ἕλκω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.4.}}
**III.2** σ. τὸ πνεῦμα **draw** breath, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Resp.” 473a2; τὸν ἀέρα τὸν κοινόν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 531.7}}; **absorb**, ἕκαστον τῶν τοῦ σώματος τὸ αὑτῷ οἰκεῖον ἐσπακέναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 930a21.}}
**III.3** _metaphorically_, **derive**, τροφήν, of winds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.38; πειθώ τε καὶ εἵμερον ἔσπασε ἐκ..}}**drew, derived..,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.889 (from Sinuessa)}}; σ. ἔρωτα **enjoy** it, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 4.270}}; ὀλίγον ὕπνου σ. **snatch** a little sleep, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.1}}:—_middle_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.16.}}
**IV** **draw tight, pull** the reins, χαλινῷ τὸ στόμα τοῦ ἵππου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.7.1, compare 9.5; but τὸν χαλινὸν ἐκ τῶν ὀδόντων ἵππου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254e.}}
**IV.2** of angling, ἡ μήρινθος οὐδὲν ἔσπασεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 928: hence proverbial, οὐκ ἔσπασεν ταύτῃ γε}} 'he took nothing by his motion', {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 175.}}
**V** **derive**, ἐπωνυμίαν παρά τινων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.10.6, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 14.15 (_middle_)}}; ἀρχὴν λυρικῆς καὶ πέρας σ. {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.184 (assuming variant)}}; ῥίζαν σ. τινός **derive** one's origin from.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 623; σ. τὴν κλῆσιν ἀπό τινος 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.46; ἔννοιαν θεοῦ ἐκ τῶν κατὰ τοὺς ὕπνους φαντασιῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 353.}}

4686:
** σπεῖρα**, ἡ,
**anything twisted** or **wound**, ποιεῖν τι οἷον σπεῖραν twist it into **a ball**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.33.}}
**2** _plural_, **coils** or **spires** of a serpent, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 535 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 500}}; πολύπλοκοι σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 481, compare “Ion” 1164}}: so in _singular_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 156, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.151, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 47, 89, etc.}}
**3** **rope, cord**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 74.21, _falsa lectio_ in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Steril.” 235}}; σπείραισι δικτυοκλώστοις with the net's meshy **folds**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 346 (Lyric poetry)}}; **ship's cable**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.507b}}; **padded circle** used by women carrying weights on their head, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 12.55}}; so by Atlas, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 2.5.11}}; as a lamp-stand, ἀρτεμισίας σ. ἐπὶ τὸν λύχνον {{Refs|“PMag.Lond.” 121.601 (compare σπειρίον 111)}}; **round cushion,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 5(1).1390.24 (Andania, 1st c.BC).}}
**4** **a mode of dressing the hair**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.31, 4.149.}}
**5** σ. βόειαι **thongs** or **straps** of ox-hide to guard and arm a boxer's fist, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.80.}}
**6** **knot** or **curl in wood**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.2.3, 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 16.198.}}
**7** a kind of _cheesecake_{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(+others σπῖρα), Chrysipp. Tyan. cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.647d.}}
**8** **rounded moulding** in the base of an Ionic or Corinthian column, **torus,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.372.64, “Sardis” 7(1) No.181 (1st c.AD), “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2713-14 (from Labranda), 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.121, 1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 3.5.3.}}
**9** Geometry texts, **anchor-ring, tore**, produced by revolution of a circle about a line in its plane but not passing through the centre, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “*Deff.” 97, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” p.119 F.}}
**II** Milit., **tactical unit**, in the Ptolemaic army, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1806.4 (1st c.BC)}}; used to translate the Roman **manipulus**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.23.1, +others, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 12.3.18, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 17}}; κατὰ σπείρας,= Latin _manipulatim_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.115.12}}; later, **cohort,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Acts.10.1, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 3.4.2, “IGRom.” 1.10 (from Massilia), 1373 (from Egypt), +others, “OGI” 208.2 (Nubia, 2nd c.AD), +others (_genitive_ in this sense always σπείρης, NT.Act.[same place], “POxy.” 477.3 (2nd c.AD), “BGU” 73 (2nd c.AD), “OGI” [same place], etc.).}}
**II.2** ={θίασος}, **religious college** or **guild**, _genitive_ σπείρης {{Refs|“IG” 14.925 (Portus Trajani); _dative_ σπείρῃ [prev. work]977 (from Rome), “Inscription Perg.” 319,320; _nominative_ σπεῖρα “AEM” 14.28 (from Roumania)}}; Latin _spira_, {{Refs|“CIL” 6.261 (from Rome), +others (compare σπειράρχης).}}

4687:
** σπείρω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **σπέρρω**{{Refs|Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus p.117 H., “Etymologicum Magnum” 300.19}}: _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ σπείρεσκον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.42}}: _future_ σπερῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 79, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 276d}}; _Aeolic dialect_ σπέρσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 202}}: _aorist_ ἔσπειρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 754 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.107, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 41c}}: _perfect_ ἔσπαρκα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 2.1.1, etc.}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ _infinitive_ σπείρασθαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1028}}; _aorist 2_ σπαρέσθαι uncertain reading in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 8.26}}:—_passive_, _future_ σπᾰρήσομαι {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Nah.1.14, (δια-) 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.69:}}_aorist_ ἐσπάρην [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1498, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.27}}: _perfect_ ἔσπαρμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1098, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1206, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 693a, etc.}}:— **sow**,
**I** **sow** seed, with _accusative_, [κέγχρους] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 399; σῖτον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.17; στάχυν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 121}}; of Cadmus, σ. γηγενῆ στάχυν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 264}} (so in _middle_, σπείρασθαι ὀδόντας {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1028)}}: _absolutely_, **sow**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 391}}; opposed to θερίζω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 710, etc.: _metaphorically_, θερ. καὶ σ. ταῖς γλώσσαις, of corrupt orators, [prev. work] 1697 (Lyric poetry)}}; καρπὸν ὧν ἔσπειρε θερίζειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 260d; αἰσχρῶς μὲν ἔσπειρας κακῶς δὲ ἐθέρισας 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista 16: proverbial, εἰς πέτρας τε καὶ λίθους σ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 838e}}; σ. κατὰ πετρῶν, i.e. εἰς πέλαγος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare (σπέρμα 1.1), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 20.}}
**I.2** **engender, beget** offspring {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare(11.2), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1293, “Tr.” 33, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 49, etc.}}; οἱ σπείραντες the **parents,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 3.1339, compare 14.1794 (from Rome)}}; ἄθυτα παλλακῶν σπέρματα σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 841d}}:—_passive_, **spring** or **be born**, ὅθενπερ αὐτὸς ἐσπάρη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1498, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 554 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 460b}}; πρὸ τοῦ Ζήνωνα.. σπαρῆναι before Z. **was begotten**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.110 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**I.3** **scatter like seed, strew**, χρυσὸν καὶ ἄργυρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.107; σ. φλόγα “Tragica Adespota” 85}}; of liquids, **scatter** or **sprinkle**, ἐκ τευχέων σ. δρόσον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 167}}; **spread abroad, extend**, σ. ἀγλαΐαν νάς ῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.13}}; **spread** rumour, σ. ματαίαν βάξιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 642}}; μὴ σπεῖρε πολλοῖς τὸν παρόντα δαίμονα **do** not **speak of** it **indiscriminately**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 653}}:—_passive_, **to be scattered** or **dispersed**, ἐσπαρμένος κατὰ.. πόλιν, of the ashes of Solon **scattered** over Salamis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 228; τόξα δ᾽ ἔσπαρται πέδῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1098}}; of persons, ἐσπάρησαν καθ᾽ Ἑλλάδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.27; ἐσπαρμένοι εἰς ἁρπαγήν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.22}}; κατὰ χώραν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 6.2.17; ἔσπαρται λόγος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 846 cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1206.}}
**II** **sow** a field, νειόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 463}}; γῆν, τέμενος, πεδιάδα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.42, 9.116, 122; ἄρουραν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 158}}; ἡ σπειρομένη Αἴγυπτος **the arable part of** Egypt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.77; τυχεῖν μὲν ἤδη 'σπαρμένα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1140; ἀροῦται καὶ σπείρεται τὸ Θηβαίων ἄστυ 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.24: proverbial, πόντον σπείρειν}}, of lost labour, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 106, 107: _metaphorically_, καινοτάταις σ. διανοίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1044; σ. εἰς ἀρετῆς ἔκφυσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 777e; τοὺς ἐν γράμμασι κήπους [prev. author] “Phdr.” 276d.}}
**II.2** of procreation, ματρὸς.. σ. ἄρουραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 754; σ. τέκνων ἄλοκα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 18; σ. λέχη [prev. author] “Ion” 64}}; ἣν ἔσπειρε, i.e. his wife, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 37.9; see above 1.2.}}

4688:
** σπεκουλάτωρ**, ορος, ὁ,
= Latin _speculator_, properly **scout**: but in the Roman Imperial army,
**1** one of the **principales** or head-quarters' staff of a legionary commander or provincial governor (whose duties included the carrying out of executions), {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Mark.6.27, “POxy.” 1193.1 (4th c.AD), etc.}}
**2** one of the Imperial body-guard (**speculatores Augusti**),= δορυφόρος, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}

4689:
** σπένδω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.775, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _subjunctive_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ σπένδῃσθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.591}}: _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ σπένδεσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.227, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.138}}: _future_ σπείσω {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Num.28.7, (κατα-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.151, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1187}}: _aorist_ ἔσπεισα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.55, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 42, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ σπεῖσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.177, σπείσασκε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.89}}; _Epic dialect_ _subjunctive_ σπείσομεν, for -ωμεν, {{Refs|7.165, 181}}: _perfect_ ἔσπεικα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|(κατ-) 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 14}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ἐσπεισάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.144, etc.}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐσπείσθην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 19:}}_perfect_ ἔσπεισμαι, see below {{Refs|11}} near the end:—**make a drink-offering** (because before drinking wine a portion was poured on the table, hearth, or altar), σπεῖσάν τ᾽ ἔπιόν θ᾽ ὅσον ἤθελε θυμός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.177; ἐπὴν σπείσῃς τε καὶ εὔξεαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.45, compare 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 1.15, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1033, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 313, etc.}}: with _dative_ of the god to whom the libation was made, σέπας ἑλὼν σπείσασκε θεοῖσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.89; ὡς σπείσῃς Διὶ πατρὶ καὶ ἄλλοις ἀθανάτοισι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.259, compare 16.227, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.334, etc.}}; τὴν (i.e. κύλικα) δὲ θεοῖς σπένδεις {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 490}}; κρητῆρας σπενδέτω probably **let** him **dedicate** them **by pouring an offering from** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 57.13 (Milet., 5th c.BC):}} —the liquid poured is mostly in _accusative_, σ. οἶνον **pour** wine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.775, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.151; λοιβάς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 270}}; σπονδάς, χοάς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 512, “Or.” 1322}}; elliptically, σ. ἀγαθοῦ δαίμονος (i.e. σπονδήν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 106}}; σ. οἴνου (_partitive_ _genitive_) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 5.5.7:}} rarely with _dative_ _of things_, ὕδατι σ. **make a drink-offering with** water, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.363}}; Ὀλυμπίῳ σ. ἀοιδαῖς **make libations with** songs to Zeus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).9: also with _dative_ instrumenti, σ. δέπαϊ, δεπάεσσι, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.196, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.137; χρυσίδι 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 124; χαλκέῃ φιάλῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.147; ἐκ χρυσέης φιάλης [prev. author] 7.54: with _genitive_, σ. βαιᾶς κύλικος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 42}}:—_passive_, οὗτος θεοῖσι σπένδεται θεὸς γεγώς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 284: so _metaphorically_ of a person, σπένδομαι ἐπὶ τῇ θυσίᾳ}} **I am offered** (**as a drink-offering**) upon it, {{NT|NT.Phil.2.17, cf. NT.2Tim.4.6.}}
**2** rarely without the religious sense, simply **pour**, τράγου οὖρον, as remedy for convulsions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.187: _metaphorically_, σ. δάκρυα “Anthologia Graeca” 7.555b (Joann.), “IG” 14.1957, +others}}; ξίφος αἷμα τυράννων ἔσπ. {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.184.}}
**II** _middle_, **pour libations one with another**, and, as this was the custom in making treaties or agreements, **make a treaty, make peace**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.144, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 199, “Av.” 1534, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.99, 119, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.7, etc.; τὰ μὲν σπενδόμενοι τὰ δὲ πολεμοῦντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.18:}} — Constr., σπένδεσθαί τινι **make peace with** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 225, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.5, etc.; in full, σπένδεσθαί τινι σπονδάς [prev. work] 14 (but σ. τῇ πρεσβείᾳ}} **give** it **pledges** of safe conduct, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.63, compare 2.109}}; so σ. τινί **obtain a truce for** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.7)}}; σ. πρός τινας, for τισι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.17, 30, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.16}}; of a person, σπείσασθαι **cum Bruto et Cassio**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 15.29.2: _metaphorically_, σ. συμφοραῖς καὶ θεσπίσμασιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1680 {σπέος}}}]; σ. ἐπὶ τοῖσδε **on these terms**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1240; ἐφ᾽ ᾧ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.4.6; also σ. ὥστε μὴ ἀδικεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.7; σ. ἦ μὴν ἐμμενεῖν ἐν ταῖς σπονδαῖς [prev. author] 4.118}}: with _infinitive_ alone, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.83: less frequently with _accusative_, εἰρήνην σπεισάμενοι Λακεδαιμονίοισι}} **having concluded a peace** with them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.148}}; νεῖκος ἐσπεῖσθαι **make up** a quarrel, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1140; πόλεμον 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.36}}; σ. ἀναίρεσιν τοῖς νεκροῖς **obtain a truce** for taking up the dead, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24; σ. σφίσιν ἡμέραν τοὺς νεκροὺς ἀνελέσθαι [prev. author] 4.114; σ. ἀναχώρησίν τινι [prev. author] 3.109; σ. περὶ τῶν τέκνων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.494d:}} —_perfect_ ἔσπεισμαι is used in _middle_ sense in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Med.” [same place]; τοῖς ἐχθροῖς ἐσπείσμην 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Phal.” 1.3}}; but in _passive_ sense, ἐσπεῖσθαι τὰς σπονδάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.16; οἷς ἔσπειστο [prev. author] 3.111}}: _aorist_, ἐσπείσθησαν ἀνοχαί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 19. (In 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.98, τοὺς νεκροὺς σπένδουσιν ἀναιρεῖσθαι}}, emphasis is laid on the act of one of the parties, and in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.422 (1st c.AD: Apollonides Epigrammaticus), σπεῖσαι δεύτερα φίλτρα γάμου, Reiske conjecture σπεύσῃς.)}} (Cf. Hittite **š** (**i**) **pand-** 'pour a libation', Latin _spondeo._)

4690:
** σπέρμ-α**, ατος, τό,
(σπείρω) **seed**, only once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, in _metaphorically_ sense, see below 1.2:}}
**I** mostly, **seed** of plants, σ. ἀνιέναι, κρύπτειν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 307, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.97: _plural_, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 446}}; σ. τῇ γῇ διδόναι, ἐμβαλεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 17.8, 10: proverbial, εἰς πέλαγος σ. βαλεῖν “epigram Gr.” 1038.8 (from Pamphylia)}}; of fruit, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 58.4}}; τοῖς γαίης σπέρμασι with **the products** of earth, of corn-stalks, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.89 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus).}}
**I.2** _metaphorically_, **germ, origin** of anything, σ. πυρός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.490; φλογός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.48, compare “P.” 3.37}}; σπέρματα, ={στοιχεῖα}, **elements**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 4, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus}}**Ep.2**p.38 U., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“fragment” 250; ὁ τὸ σπέρμα παρασχών, οὗτος τῶν φύντων αἴτιος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.159; συκοφάντου σ. καὶ ῥίζαν οἴεται δεῖν ὑπάρχειν τῇ πόλει [prev. author] 25.48; σ. τῆς στάσεως 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 10; τοῦ ὅρκου 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 16.3.}}
**I.3** **seed-time, sowing**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 781.}}
**II** of animals, **seed, semen**, φέροισα σ. θεοῦ pregnant **by** the god, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.15}}; but σ. φέρειν Ἡρακλέους to be pregnant **of** Heracles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “N.” 10.17; μυελὸν.. εἰς σ. καὶ γόνον μερίζεσθαι 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 100b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 86c; σ. παραλαβεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 553; σπέρματος πλῆσαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 15: _plural_, κατ᾽ ἀμφότερα τὰ σ. θεῶν ἀπόγονος 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 2.}}
**II.2** **race, origin, descent**, τοὐμὸν.. σπέρμ᾽ ἰδεῖν βουλήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1077}}; τίνος εἶ σπέρματος πατρόθε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 214 (Lyric poetry); γένεθλον σπέρμα τ᾽ Ἀργεῖον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 290, compare “Ch.” 236; σ. ἄντασ᾽ Ἐρεχθειδᾶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 981 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.93, etc.}}
**II.3** frequently in Poets, **seed, offspring**, τὸ βρότειον σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 399; σ. Πελοπιδῶν [prev. author] “Ch.” 503}}; σ. [τοῦ Ἀβραάμ] {{NT|NT.Luke.1.55, etc.}}; sometimes of a single person, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.61, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 705, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 364, Oracle texts cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.16, LXX.Gen.4.25, etc.: _plural_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 803, 909, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1246, “OC” 600, NT.Gal.3.16; once in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, ἀνθρώπων σπέρμασι νουθετοῦμεν “Lg.” 853c.}}

4691:
** σπερμο-λόγος**, ον,
(λέγω) **picking up seeds**, of birds, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Demetr.” 28, Alex.Mynd. cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.388a}}: as _substantive_, **rook**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 592b28}}; glossed κολοιῶδες ζῷον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; σπερμολόγων τε γένη 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 232 (Lyric poetry), compare 579.}}
**2** τὰ σπερμολόγα τῶν παιδαρίων **guttersnipes**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.85f.}}
**II** **picking up scraps, gossiping**, ἄνθρωπος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 19.5 (_superlative_, [prev. work] 4)}}; also πικρὰ καὶ σ. ῥήματα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.456c.}}
**III** as _substantive_, **one who picks up and retails scraps of knowledge, an idle babbler, gossip**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.127, NT.Act.17.18, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.344c.}}

4692:
** σπεύδω**,
_Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ σπευδέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.324}}: _future_ σπεύσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 153 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 926, etc.; Cretan dialect σπευσίω “SIG” 527.42 (3rd c.BC)}}: _aorist_ ἔσπευσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 161, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45c, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ σπεῦσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.250}}; _subjunctive_ σπεύσομεν, for -ωμεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.121}}: _perfect_ ἔσπευκα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Annuario” 8 /9.375 (Perga, 2nd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 7.15.11}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 151 (Lyric poetry)}}: _future_ σπεύσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.402}}:— _passive_, _perfect_ ἔσπευσμαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 33, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.895.}}
**I** _transitive_, **set going, urge on, hasten**, ταῦτα δ᾽ ἅμα χρὴ σπεύδειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.236; οἱ δὲ γάμον σπεύδουσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.137, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.38}}; παῦσαι σπεύδων τὰ σπεύδεις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 206; σ. ἀθλίαν ὁδόν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1226}}; σ. οἱ μὲν ἴγδιν, οἱ δὲ σίλφιον, οἱ δ᾽ ὄξος **procure quickly, get ready**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 39; κλίμακας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1352}}; σπευσίω ὅτι κα δύναμαι κακὸν τᾷ πόλει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” [prev. cited] (in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.46, Δημοκρίτου σπεύσαντος, an _accusative_ must be supplied).}}
**I.b** **seek eagerly, strive after**, μηδὲν ἄγαν σ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 335, 401}}; σ. βίον ἀθάνατον, μακροτέραν ἀρετάν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.62, “I.” 4(3).13(31); εὐψυχίαν ἀντ᾽ εὐβουλίας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 161; τὴν ἡγεμονίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.16; χάριν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1175; πόλεμον τέκνοις [prev. author] “HF” 1133.}}
**I.c** **promote** or **further zealously, press** or **urge on**, τι τῶν φέρει φρήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 599 (Lyric poetry); τὸ σὸν σ. ἅμα καὶ τοὐμόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 251; τὸ σὸν ἀγαθόν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 120 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὸ ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτὸν ἕκαστος σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141; σ. ἀσπούδαστ᾽ ἐπὶ σοὶ δαίμων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 201 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὰ ἐναντία τῇ ἑαυτῶν ὠφελείᾳ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.2}}; in arguing, σεαυτῷ τὰ ἐναντία σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 361a}}; σ. τοῦτο, ὅπως.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 687e; μὴ σπεῦδ᾽ ἃ μὴ δεῖ, μηδ᾽ ἃ δεῖ σπεύδειν μένε 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 344: with _dative_, οἱ Χαιρέᾳ σπεύδοντες}} the **partisans of** Chaereas, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 6.1: ἐς τὰ Ἑλλήνων σ. 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.8}}: followed by a _conjunctive_, εἰς τοὺς πλουσίους σπεύσω σ᾽ ὅπως ἂν ἐγγραφῇς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 926}}:—_middle_, σπευδομένα θυσίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 151 (Lyric poetry)}}: —_passive_, ξυνὸν πᾶσι ἀγαθὸν σπεύδεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.53}}; ἐσπευσμέναι χρεῖαι **pressing** needs, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 33.}}
**I.2** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, σπεύσατε.. Τεῦκρον ἐν τάχει μολεῖν **urge** him to come quickly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 804}}; σπεῦσον.. κάπετόν τιν᾽ ἰδεῖν **hasten** to look out for.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 1165 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II** more frequently _intransitive_, **press on, hasten.** διὰ δρυμὰ πυκνὰ καὶ ὕλην σπεύδουσ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.119, compare 8.191, 23.414, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 228}}; σ. ἀπὸ ῥυτῆρος with loose rein, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 900; δρόμῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1556; πεζῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.49, etc.}}; **exert oneself, strive eagerly** or **anxiously**, of warriors fighting, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.232, compare 8.293, etc.}}; of a smith at work, {{Refs|18.373}}; of beasts of draught, {{Refs|17.745}}; of bees working, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 597: proverbial, ὅταν σπεύδῃ τις αὐτὸς χὠ θεὸς συνάπτεται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 742 (troch.)}}; σπεῦδε βραδέως **festina lente**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Gellius, Aulus Grammaticus Latinus 10.11.5}}; σ. τινί **exert oneself for** another, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 309:}} —Construct.,
**II.1** with _participle_, σπεῦσε πονησάμενος τὰ ἃ ἔργα (for σπουδαίως ἐπονήσατο) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.250, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 935, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 761 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 179: reversely, σπεύδων}} **in haste, eagerly**, τὼ δὲ σπεύδοντε πετέσθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.506; ἵκετο σπεύδων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.95; εἰς ἀρθμὸν ἐμοὶ.. σπεύδων σπεύδοντί ποθ᾽ ἥξει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 193 (_anapaest meter_); σ. ἐβοήθει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.3.1.}}
**II.2** with _infinitive_, **to be eager to**.., Hes {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Op.” 22,673, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.11(14), “N.” 9.21, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 601, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.41; σῴζειν θέλοντας ἄνδρα γ᾽ ὃς σπεύδῃ θανεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 812}}:—_middle_, σπευδομένα ἀφελεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 360 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **to be anxious that**.., εἰρήνην ἑωυτοῖσι γενέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.74; ἔσπευδεν εἶναι μὴ μάχας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 672, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 361a}}; τὸ λεκτικοὺς γίγνεσθαι τοὺς συνόντας οὐκ ἔσπ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.1: also ἔσπευσεν τοῦ διατηρηθῆναι τὴν εὐφημίαν αὐτοῖς “IG” 22.1028.83.}}
**II.4** followed by ὡς, ὅπως, etc., σ. ὡς Ζεὺς μήποτ᾽ ἄρξειεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 205}}; σ. ὅπως μὴ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 480b}}; ἵνα.., ἵνα μὴ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Plt.” 264a, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.164}}; ὥστε μή, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 57.}}
**II.5** followed by a _preposition_, σ. μάχην ἐς **show eagerness** for.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.225 (_middle_, σπεύσομαι εἰς Ἀχιλῆα, ἵνα}}.. **hasten**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.402); εἰς ἄφενος σπεύδειν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 24; εἰς ἀρετήν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 403; ἐς θαλάμους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 182 (_anapaest meter_); ἐς τὰ πράγματα [prev. author] “Ion” 599, etc.; εἰς τὸ αὐτὸ ἡμῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.4; δώματος εἴσω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 100 (_anapaest meter_); ἐπί τι 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 57}}; περὶ Πατρόκλοιο θανόντος **struggle** for him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.121; ὑπέρ τινος “IG” 12(9).903 (Chalcis, 2nd c.BC)}}; πρός τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1026, etc.; also σ. ὁδόν “IG” 14.1729.}}
**II.6** with _adverb_, σ. οἷ θέλεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 334; δεῦρο 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 179; ἔνθα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.14, etc.}}
**II.7** **to be troubled in mind, harassed**, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.15.15, LXX.1Ki.28.21, +others (Cf. σπουδή, σπούδαξ}}, Lithuanian _spáudžiu_ 'press'.)

4693:
** σπήλαιον**, τό,
**grotto, cavern**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 514a, 515a, 539e, 4th c.BC: Moschio Tragicus Trag. 6.5, 1st c.BC(?): Satyrus Epigrammaticus “Vit.Eur.fragment” 39 ix 6, NT.Rev.6.15, etc.}}; σ. λῃστῶν **den** of robbers, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Jer.7.11, NT.Matt.21.13}}; of a **grave,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|Supp.epigram 7.160 (Palmyra, 1st c.AD), 166 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.AD), NT.John.11.38.}}
**2** **privy parts**, {{LXX|LXX.Hab.2.15 (_plural_).}}
**3** **place behind the scenes** in a theatre, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.124.}}

4694:
** σπῐλάς**, άδος, ἡ,
**A** **rock over which the sea dashes** (opposed to ὕφαλοι πέτραι in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.390 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)), νῆάς γε ποτὶ σπιλάδεσσιν ἔαξαν κύματα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.298; δοῦπον ἄκουσε ποτὶ σπιλάδεσσι θαλάσσης 5.401 (see. πάγος)}}; ἐφ᾽ ὑψηλαῖς σπιλάδεσσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 371 (Lyric poetry)}}; πλαγκταὶ σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.932; σ. εἰν ἁλὶ πέτρη [prev. author] 3.1294}}; ῥεῖθρον ἀπὸ σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Epigrammata” 4.6}}: generally, **slab**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 678}}; ὧδ᾽ ὑπὸ τὸ σπιλάδος μέλαθρον, i.e. under this **tombstone,** {{Refs|Sammelb. 6160 (from Egypt)}}; **hollow rock, cave**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus (?) 179.}}
**A.II** as _adjective_, **stony**, or perhaps **marly**, i.e. γῆ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.4.4.}}

4697:
** σπλαγχν-ίζω**,
= σπλαγχνεύω, {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.6.8}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Abh.Berl.Akad.” 1928(6).12 (Cos, 4th c.BC)}}.
**II** _middle_ (with _aorist_ _passive_ -ίσθην), **feel pity, compassion**, or **mercy**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Pro.17.5; ἐπί τινα NT.Mark.6.34, 8.2, etc.; περί τινων NT.Matt.9.36}}; οὐκ ἐσπλαγχνίσθη **had** no **mercy,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PFlor. 296.23 (6th c.AD).}}

4698:
** σπλάγχνον**, τό,
mostly in _plural_ σπλάγχνα (σπλάγχανα {{Refs|“SIG” 1002)}}, **inward parts**, especially the heart, lungs, liver, kidneys, which in sacrifices were reserved to be eaten by the sacrificers at the beginning of their feast (distinguished from ἔντερα, κοιλίη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1221, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.40, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 667b3)}}; σπλάγχνα πάσαντο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|lines 1.464, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.9}}; δῶκε δ᾽ ἄρα σπλάγχνων μοίρας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 40; σπλάγχνα δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ σπτήσαντες ἐνώμων 20.252, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1105 (hexameters)}}: hence, **sacrificial feast**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eq.” 410, “V.” 654, “SIG” 1002.4 (Milet., 5th-4th c.BC), 1044.39 (Halic., 4th-3rd c.BC), “Test.Epict.” 6.17, etc.; also as used in divination, σπλάγχνων τε λειότητα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 493, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 212, “El.” 828, 838, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.160.}}
**2** **any part of the inwards**, ὑπὸ σπλάγχνων ἐλθεῖν to come from the **womb**, of a babe, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.43, compare “N.” 1.35; τῶν σῶν.. ἐκ σπλάγχνων ἕνα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1066; μητρὸς ἐν σπλάγχνοις “IG” 14.1977}}: so in _singular_, τὸ κοινὸν σ. οὗ πεφύκαμεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1036}}; of the lungs, μόχθοις ἀνδροκμῆσι φυσιᾷ σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 249}}; τοῦ γείτονος αὐτῷ (i.e. τῷ ἥπατι) σπλάγχνου, of the spleen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 72c.}}
**3** οἱ παῖδες (**children**) σπλάγχνα λέγονται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.44, compare 5.57.}}
**II** _metaphorically_ (like **heart**), **the seat of the feelings, affections**, especially of anger, σ. θερμῆναι κότῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 844}}; τὰ σ. ἀγανακτεῖ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1006; μομφὰς ὑπὸ σπλάγχνοις ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1009: generally, of anxiety, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 995 (Lyric poetry); σπλάγχνα δέ μου κελαινοῦται [prev. author] “Ch.” 413 (Lyric poetry)}}; of love, ἐκύμηνε τὰ σ. ἔρωτι καρδίην ἀνοιστρηθείς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.56; παιδὸς ὑπὸ σπλάγχνοισιν ἔχει πόθον 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.99, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.35, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.55 (3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus)}}, etc.; of pity, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Pro.12.10, NT.Phil.1.8, 2.1, etc.}}; ὑπὲρ σπλάχνου 'for **pity's** sake', {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1139.17 (1st c.BC)}}; so {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 995, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1201, “Hipp.” 118}}; ἀνδρὸς σ. ἐκμαθεῖν to learn a man's '**heart**', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Med.” 220; ἀνδρὸς πονηροῦ σ. οὐ μαλάσσεται 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 31}}; σ. σιδηροῦν, of Epaminondas, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 560.}}
**III** ={βρύον}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.21.}}

4699:
** σπόγγος**, ὁ,
**sponge**, σ. πολυτρήτοισι τραπέζας νίζον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.111, compare 22.439; σπόγγῳ ἀμφὶ πρόσωπα καὶ ἄμφω χεῖρ᾽ ἀπομόργνυ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.414; ὑγρώσσων σ. ὤλεσεν γραφήν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1329}}; used at the bath, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 55, 5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 15.7}}; for cleaning shoes. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 600 (compare σπογγίζω);compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 487b9, 588b20, NT.Matt.27.48, etc.}}
**II** **any spongy substance**, σικυώνης σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Steril.” 221}}; οἱ σ. the **glands** in the throat, **tonsils**, from their **spongy** nature and liability to swell, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Epid.” 4.7, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.140.}}
**III** ={νήριον}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.81}}. (**σφόγγος** is found in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 11 (2). 144 A 37 (Delos, 4th c.BC), “PSI” 6.558.7 (3rd c.BC), “POxy.” 1384.25 (V 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus)}}; compare σπογγιά, σπογγίον.)
